 of policy issues at X and the 2024 General Elections in India. Prior to joining Forbes, Siladitya worked as a reporter with the Hindustan Times and Medianama covering tech policy and consumer tech in India. He graduated from Columbia University with an MA in Business and Economics Journalism in 2019. Follow Ray for continued coverage on TikTok in the U.S., Elon Musk and X and other key developments at big tech companies. Tips:siladitya@protonmail.com. Forbes reporters follow company ethical guidelines that ensure the highest quality.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/603dbb9c817c79d6f5f9aa66/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1080\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=1080","authorSlug":"siladityaray","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Duane \"Keffe D\" Davis was found guilty of first-degree murder in the 1996 killing of rap icon Tupac Shakur, nearly 30 years after the infamous Las Vegas drive-by shooting. Davis, 63, faces a life sentence without parole, with sentencing set for October 13. He immediately vowed to appeal the verdict, raising his fist as he exited the courtroom. Prosecutors argued Davis, a Southside Compton Crips leader, orchestrated the murder, acting as the \"shot caller\" after his nephew was beaten by Shakur and Suge Knight. They emphasized Davis's own public confessions in books and interviews as direct evidence, alongside circumstantial evidence and witnesses. The defense countered, citing a lack of physical evidence and inconsistencies in Davis's accounts, claiming his book was fiction. Davis is the first and only person charged in the case, despite not being accused of pulling the trigger himself. Tupac's sister, Sekyiwa Shakur, was visibly emotional in court, hugging prosecutors after the verdict.","shortHeadline":"Tupac Murder Verdict","topic":"Crime \u0026 Justice","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/50531-6a965e66e5a5811a7bafd70e"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/EHhEb6ix-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"EHhEb6ix","audioDurationS":310,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-09-01T06:30:42-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-08-30-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-08-30","script":"Welcome to The Daily Brief, a look at today's top reporting from Forbes. It's Sunday, August 30. On the agenda today: the latest developments in Real Estate, Politics, and Immigration.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nDiving into the Real Estate space, we start with, Billionaire tim draper selling his private island in tanzania—wants offers by email. According to reporting from Mary Whitfill Roeloffs\n\nVenture capital billionaire Tim Draper is selling his 110-acre private island, Lupita Island, located on Lake Tanganyika in Tanzania, for an asking price of $7.9 million. Draper, a prominent investor in companies like Tesla and SpaceX and a significant cryptocurrency holder, announced the sale on X. Draper acquired the island in 2004, subsequently investing four years into developing it with ten luxurious cottages, a spa, gym, game room, multiple bars, and a swimming pool. The exclusive retreat offers a range of activities, including cruising on a 46-foot boat, swimming at a private beach, exploring ancient monastery ruins, kayaking, snorkeling, fishing, and arranging safaris on mainland Tanzania. The island, which has been available for rent, provides guests with a private butler and a dedicated staff of over 30. Access to Lupita Island involves a complex journey, typically requiring multiple flights, either via a private jet to the island's own airstrip or a commercial flight followed by a car and boat transfer. Draper, with an estimated net worth of $2.3 billion, famously profited from a large Bitcoin purchase from the Silk Road auction in 2014.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nFor more context on that story, visit Forbes.com. Next, we shift our focus to, RFK jr. says pennsylvania lied about ‘so-called’ measles deaths, a story that could have significant implications for Politics.\n\nPennsylvania has become the epicenter of a high-profile political battle following two \"measles-associated\" deaths, the state's first in 35 years. Democratic Governor Josh Shapiro accused Health Secretary Robert F. Kennedy Jr. of contributing to vaccine misinformation. Kennedy, however, dismissed the deaths as \"so-called\" or potentially \"fabricated,\" claiming a county official denied measles as the cause. He further accused Shapiro of employing a \"COVID playbook\" by allegedly inflating death tolls, similar to how he believes pandemic deaths were counted. One infant's death was attributed to a lacerated spleen but was deemed \"measles-associated\" due to a positive measles test, as the virus can make the spleen vulnerable. Shapiro has withheld further details, citing patient privacy, which has fueled Kennedy's skepticism about direct causation. This dispute has ignited a broader public health debate, largely along partisan lines, with Democrats decrying dangerous vaccine skepticism and Republicans criticizing state health accounting as alarmist overreach. Kennedy, as Health Secretary, has advocated for significant changes to federal vaccine policy, including reassessing childhood immunization schedules and exploring splitting the MMR vaccine. The controversy also coincides with Pennsylvania's midterm elections, with Shapiro's Republican opponent leveraging the issue against him.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nOur final story today looks at, Far-Right provocateur milo yiannopoulos deported by ICE. This remains a major point of conversation in the world of Immigration.\n\nFar-right political activist Milo Yiannopoulos was arrested by Immigration and Customs Enforcement in New Orleans on Thursday and remains in custody, the Department of Homeland Security confirmed. DHS stated Yiannopoulos, a UK native living in the U.S., entered legally in May 2019 but overstayed his welcome and failed to attend an immigration hearing, leading to a removal order issued July 22. He will stay in ICE custody pending his removal. Yiannopoulos previously claimed to hold an O-1 \"extraordinary ability\" visa. His immigration status reportedly gained attention after a falling out with senior Trump administration officials. Fellow far-right activist Laura Loomer claimed credit for his detainment, alleging Yiannopoulos worked for former Rep. Marjorie Taylor Greene and lived in her home. TMZ first reported the arrest, noting Yiannopoulos was in New Orleans for a Kanye West concert, having previously worked as a consultant for West. Ironically, Yiannopoulos has been a vocal proponent of strict anti-immigration policies, advocating for the deportation of millions. He rose to prominence before the 2016 election, known for controversial articles and college tours, and was banned from Twitter for harassment, later resigning from Breitbart in 2017 after controversial comments.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou're all caught up with The Daily Brief. Join us again tomorrow for your next news digest. Thanks for joining us.","scriptTemplateId":2,"articles":[{"title":"Billionaire Tim Draper Selling His Private Island In Tanzania—Wants Offers By Email","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/maryroeloffs/2026/08/29/billionaire-tim-draper-selling-his-private-island-in-tanzania-wants-offers-by-email/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a92ff8b4d06ca14ac4bd565/960x0.jpg","author":"Mary Whitfill Roeloffs","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/7236790","authorUrl":"https://www.forbes.com/sites/maryroeloffs","authorBio":"Mary Roeloffs is a Forbes reporter who covers breaking news with a frequent focus on the entertainment industry, streaming, pop culture, politics, public health and climate change. She joined Forbes in 2023 and lives in Rochester, N.Y. She’s covered Hollywood’s most contentious lawsuits, allegations against Sean “Diddy” Combs, the Trump administration’s controversies, how AI is changing the job marke