cos in 2020 and completed a master’s degree in journalism at the University of Southern California the following year. Follow Pequeño for continued coverage on Elon Musk, X and ongoing developments at various social media companies. Tips: apequeno@forbes.com.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/645b37db4787b6f745b32fd0/400x0.jpg?cropX1=41\u0026cropX2=581\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=539","authorSlug":"antoniopequenoiv","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Far-right political activist Milo Yiannopoulos was arrested by Immigration and Customs Enforcement in New Orleans on Thursday and remains in custody, the Department of Homeland Security confirmed. DHS stated Yiannopoulos, a UK native living in the U.S., entered legally in May 2019 but overstayed his welcome and failed to attend an immigration hearing, leading to a removal order issued July 22. He will stay in ICE custody pending his removal. Yiannopoulos previously claimed to hold an O-1 \"extraordinary ability\" visa. His immigration status reportedly gained attention after a falling out with senior Trump administration officials. Fellow far-right activist Laura Loomer claimed credit for his detainment, alleging Yiannopoulos worked for former Rep. Marjorie Taylor Greene and lived in her home. TMZ first reported the arrest, noting Yiannopoulos was in New Orleans for a Kanye West concert, having previously worked as a consultant for West. Ironically, Yiannopoulos has been a vocal proponent of strict anti-immigration policies, advocating for the deportation of millions. He rose to prominence before the 2016 election, known for controversial articles and college tours, and was banned from Twitter for harassment, later resigning from Breitbart in 2017 after controversial comments.","shortHeadline":"Yiannopoulos Deportation","topic":"Immigration","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51846-6a91d74e7cb760c376a51656"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/ltX0lYSQ-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"ltX0lYSQ","audioDurationS":324,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-08-30T08:30:43-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-08-29-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-08-29","script":"You're listening to The Daily Brief, your essential news update from Forbes. It's Saturday, August 29. Today, we're highlighting the key stories covering Travel, Travel, and a closer look at International Living.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nLeading off with, U.S. destinations woo canadians with discounts amid trump’s tariff war. Suzanne Rowan Kelleher has been tracking this development and reports that\n\nAmidst ongoing tariff disputes initiated by President Trump, the U.S. tourism industry is aggressively campaigning to lure back Canadian visitors, who have significantly reduced their travel south of the border. Statistics Canada reports a persistent shift away from the U.S. by Canadian residents, with a 22% drop in visitors in 2025, equating to four million fewer travelers and an estimated $4.5 billion economic hit. This decline began in 2025 following political tensions and former Prime Minister Trudeau's advice against U.S. vacations. Major destinations like New York, Las Vegas, and Florida have seen substantial drops in Canadian spending and visitation. In response, U.S. states and cities are rolling out extensive promotions, including deep hotel discounts, at-par exchange rates, and special packages.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nRead the full report on that story at Forbes.com. Moving now to, 16 stunning fall getaways to book right now, a story that's currently shaping the landscape in Travel.\n\nFollowing a record-hot summer, fall offers a welcome shift, inviting travelers to embrace cooler temperatures and vibrant global experiences. North America offers classic autumn splendor, from New England's foliage and outdoor activities to Montana's glamping and harvest meals. For warmer escapes, Florida provides family packages, while Mexico hosts culinary festivals. European destinations like Tuscany and Greece invite guests to partake in grape and olive harvests; Finland and London offer stunning fall colors and urban exploration. Asian highlights include Shenzhen's clear weather and coastal walks, plus Hong Kong's Mid-Autumn Festival views and cultural events. Unique international experiences range from safaris in Kenya to wine harvest tours in Chile amidst amber trees. Boston provides a base for exploring autumn hues and the Freedom Trail, while Arizona offers celestial wellness and stargazing. Japan showcases golden ginkgo trees and cultural sites, completing a diverse array of global autumn getaways for every preference.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nTo finish today's update, we're turning to, 3 overseas alternatives to san diego: similar lifestyles for far less. This headline is generating significant interest within International Living.\n\nSan Diego offers an enviable lifestyle with year-round sunshine, Pacific beaches, and a vibrant culture, yet it's one of America's most expensive cities. The cost of living is 62% above the national average, with median home prices reaching $1.25 million and high property taxes. This financial burden makes the San Diego dream untenable for many. However, attractive overseas destinations offer similar lifestyles at a fraction of the cost. For instance, Playa Venao, Panama, a popular surf town, provides a sophisticated beach life. A modern 3-bedroom ocean-view home there costs $575,000, with significantly lower property taxes and living expenses. Setúbal, Portugal, dubbed \"Eurofornia,\" offers warm weather, Atlantic beaches, and nature. A spacious 3-bedroom apartment is priced at $486,990, benefiting from lower living costs and property taxes. Finally, Essaouira, Morocco, a laid-back Atlantic coastal city with a UNESCO Medina, presents a 4-bedroom traditional riad for just $425,205, coupled with a very low cost of living. These locations allow San Diego homeowners to unlock substantial equity while enjoying a comparable quality of life.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nThat wraps up this edition of The Daily Brief. Be sure to check back tomorrow for our next update. Thanks for listening.","scriptTemplateId":1,"articles":[{"title":"U.S. Destinations Woo Canadians With Discounts Amid Trump’s Tariff War","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/suzannerowankelleher/2026/08/28/us-destinations-woo-canadians-discounts-tariff-war/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a91ed834f36dc544e80a007/960x0.jpg","author":"Suzanne Rowan Kelleher","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/3340630","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/suzannerowankelleher/","authorBio":"Suzanne Rowan Kelleher covers travel for Forbes. Follow her on Bluesky @srkelleher.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/601b7235b35a3ce5939c28fe/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1080\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=1080","authorSlug":"suzannerowankelleher","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Amidst ongoing tariff disputes initiated by President Trump, the U.S. tourism industry is aggressively campaigning to lure back Canadian visitors, who have significantly reduced their travel south of the border. Statistics Canada reports a persistent shift away from the U.S. by Canadian residents, with a 22% drop in visitors in 2025, equating to four million fewer travelers and an estimated $4.5 billion economic hit. This decline began in 2025 following political tensions and former Prime Minister Trudeau's advice against U.S. vacations. Major destinations like New York, Las Vegas, and Florida have seen substantial drops in Canadian spending and visitation. In response, U.S. states and cities are rolling out extensive promotions, including deep hotel discounts, at-par exchange rates, and special packages.","shortHeadline":"Travel Discounts","topic":"Travel","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/7082-6a91eb01aad44007973d6678"},{"title":"16 Stunni