eports that\n\nA newly published letter from Health Secretary Robert F. Kennedy Jr. to Samoa's prime minister in 2019 directly contradicts his congressional testimony, revealing his intent to meddle in the nation's measles, mumps, and rubella (MMR) vaccine use. Kennedy had previously claimed his visit was solely for a medical informatics system, but the letter explicitly mentions studying adverse effects from a \"defective batch\" of MMR vaccine. This trip is controversially linked to Samoa's devastating 2019 measles outbreak, which sickened over 5,700 people and killed 83. Samoa's Director-General of Health, Dr. Alec Ekeroma, accused Kennedy of misleading the Senate and fueling a \"significant disinformation campaign\" that emboldened anti-vaccine activists, despite earlier infant deaths being due to medical malpractice, not vaccine defect. Kennedy's visit coincided with Samoa's vaccination rates plummeting from 84% to 31%. As Health Secretary, Kennedy has since advocated for broad changes to federal vaccine policy, including altering CDC statements on vaccines and autism, and reassessing the childhood immunization schedule. President Trump recently signed an executive order to reduce recommended childhood immunizations and explore splitting the MMR vaccine. Nationally, U.S. MMR vaccination rates for kindergartners have fallen to 92.4%, coinciding with a historic spike of over 2,700 measles cases in 2026.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou can find the full analysis of that story at Forbes.com. Now, we're turning our attention to, Up to 90 american tourists thought missing in nepal floods—here’s why accurate numbers are hard to come by, a development that could have a lasting impact on Disasters.\n\nCatastrophic flash floods in the Nepal-Tibet border region have killed at least 389 people, with 667, including 65 Americans, reported missing by the Nepal Tourism Board. The U.S. State Department initially stated 90 Americans were \"unaccounted for\" across China and Nepal, confirming no known U.S. deaths. The disaster, caused by a landslide linked to a glacial event, sent a destructive water surge downstream. A significant challenge for the U.S. government is accurately accounting for its citizens abroad during crises, as there is no real-time registry. The State Department relies on fragmented information from families, local authorities, and travel operators, a process proven difficult in past events like the 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami and 2024 Haiti crisis. Despite encouraging enrollment in the voluntary Smart Traveler Enrollment Program (STEP), participation remains low, hindering accurate counts.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nRounding out today's briefing, we look at, Trump signs executive order renaming ‘lake ontario’ as ‘lake america’. This story is currently at the center of the conversation in Policy.\n\nPresident Donald Trump signed an executive order Thursday, officially renaming \"Lake Ontario\" to \"Lake America,\" fulfilling a threat issued earlier this week amid a deepening trade war with Canada. Trump, who stated he had been contemplating the change \"for a long time,\" directed the Interior Secretary to update federal geographic systems and documents within 30 days. This action follows Trump's social media posts and a video where he crossed out the old name, asserting \"we don't expect to be doing much business with Ontario any longer.\" The renaming directly responds to Canada's planned retaliatory tariffs, announced after the U.S. imposed 50% levies on approximately $20 billion in Canadian goods, including alcohol, dairy, and cars. While the tariffs' economic impact is expected to be minimal for both nations, Canada's targeted tariffs aim to exert political pressure on the Trump administration by affecting states with competitive midterm elections. Trump previously renamed the \"Gulf of Mexico\" to the \"Gulf of America\" in January 2025 and has hinted at future ocean name changes.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou've reached the end of this Daily Brief. Tune in again for tomorrow's updates. Thanks for joining us.","scriptTemplateId":0,"articles":[{"title":"RFK Jr. Lied About Famous Samoan Trip During Confirmation Hearings, New Letter Indicates","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/maryroeloffs/2026/08/27/rfk-jr-lied-to-about-famous-samoan-trip-during-confirmation-hearings-new-letter-indicates/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a9030fba93cbdba70290b9f/960x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=2702\u0026cropY1=104\u0026cropY2=1623","author":"Mary Whitfill Roeloffs","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/7236790","authorUrl":"https://www.forbes.com/sites/maryroeloffs","authorBio":"Mary Roeloffs is a Forbes reporter who covers breaking news with a frequent focus on the entertainment industry, streaming, pop culture, politics, public health and climate change. She joined Forbes in 2023 and lives in Rochester, N.Y. She’s covered Hollywood’s most contentious lawsuits, allegations against Sean “Diddy” Combs, the Trump administration’s controversies, how AI is changing the job market and the 2026 hantavirus outbreak. Roeloffs was included on Editor \u0026 Publisher Magazine’s “25 Under 30” list in 2023 and worked covering local news in the greater Boston area from 2017 to 2023. She graduated with a double major in political science and journalism from Northeastern University. Follow Roeloffs for continued coverage of streaming wars, pop culture news and trending topics.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6487561380bb7d9077f4b945/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=400\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=400","authorSlug":"maryroeloffs","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"A newly published letter from Health Secretary Robert F. Kennedy Jr. to Samoa's prime minister in 2019 directly contradicts his congressional testimony, revealing his intent to meddle in the nation's measles, mumps, and rubella (MMR) vaccine use. K