d classified documents cases and his Manhattan hush money case. Dorn graduated in 2012 from the University of Dayton with a degree in journalism. Prior to joining Forbes, she covered New York City and state politics for the New York Post and City and State magazine. Follow her for updates and analysis on the 2024 presidential race, key Senate and House races and developments in Congress and at the White House.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/63457b718c2404f82daa3149/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1284\u0026cropY1=403\u0026cropY2=1687","authorSlug":"saradorn","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"President Donald Trump signed an executive order Thursday, officially renaming \"Lake Ontario\" to \"Lake America,\" fulfilling a threat issued earlier this week amid a deepening trade war with Canada. Trump, who stated he had been contemplating the change \"for a long time,\" directed the Interior Secretary to update federal geographic systems and documents within 30 days. This action follows Trump's social media posts and a video where he crossed out the old name, asserting \"we don't expect to be doing much business with Ontario any longer.\" The renaming directly responds to Canada's planned retaliatory tariffs, announced after the U.S. imposed 50% levies on approximately $20 billion in Canadian goods, including alcohol, dairy, and cars. While the tariffs' economic impact is expected to be minimal for both nations, Canada's targeted tariffs aim to exert political pressure on the Trump administration by affecting states with competitive midterm elections. Trump previously renamed the \"Gulf of Mexico\" to the \"Gulf of America\" in January 2025 and has hinted at future ocean name changes.","shortHeadline":"Presidential Name Changes","topic":"Policy","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51554-6a9076c674b24b7f17c4feed"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/QFtC2zWT-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"QFtC2zWT","audioDurationS":295,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-08-28T06:30:44-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-08-27-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-08-27","script":"Welcome to The Daily Brief, a look at today's top reporting from Forbes. It's Thursday, August 27. On the agenda today: the latest developments in Visa Policy, Trade Policy, and Medical Innovation.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nDiving into the Visa Policy space, we start with, Global visa interviews on hold—history shows a 36% drop may follow. According to reporting from Mary Whitfill Roeloffs\n\nThe Trump administration has ordered a global halt on all visa applications and canceled scheduled interviews at U.S. embassies, aiming to tighten restrictions and significantly reduce the number of visas issued. This pause is intended for \"in-depth training\" of consular officers, equipping them to reject applicants deemed potential financial burdens on the government. The move follows a judge's recent ruling against the administration's attempt to ban immigration applications from 75 mostly poorer countries, whose citizens were considered likely to need public assistance. Historically, similar pauses have led to substantial drops in visa issuance once processing resumes, disproportionately affecting some nations. The visa action aligns with the administration's broader immigration crackdown, despite data indicating immigrants generally use public safety-net programs at lower rates than native-born Americans. The State Department has not yet announced when visa appointments will resume.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nFor more context on that story, visit Forbes.com. Next, we shift our focus to, Trump shares rant about GOP ‘F- - -ing up’ as republicans criticize his latest tariff moves, a story that could have significant implications for Trade Policy.\n\nPresident Donald Trump recently engaged in a wide-ranging social media spree, sharing a post that sharply criticized the Republican Party for \"f---ing up\" and \"sleepwalking toward a midterm reckoning.\" This came amidst significant blowback over his controversial trade policies, including a renewed trade war with Canada and relaxed restrictions on certain beef imports. Trump's online activity, which spanned topics from personal accomplishments and family photos to slamming the Baseball Hall of Fame and criticizing a Republican congressman for endorsing Nikki Haley, coincided with growing concerns about the GOP's prospects in the upcoming midterm elections. His decision to impose 50% tariffs on Canadian goods, prompting retaliation, and to ease beef import rules has angered Republicans in key border and agricultural states. These trade actions, coupled with Trump's consistently low approval ratings—hovering around 33-38% and near a second-term low—and ongoing economic anxieties, are seen as threatening the Republican Party's electoral success. Polls indicate a shift, with more voters now trusting Democrats on economic issues, further complicating the GOP's path to victory.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nOur final story today looks at, FDA approves pancreatic cancer drug that could double survival time. This remains a major point of conversation in the world of Medical Innovation.\n\nThe Food and Drug Administration has approved daraxonrasib, sold as Rasonque by Revolution Medicines, a new once-daily pill for metastatic pancreatic cancer. This first-in-class targeted therapy effectively blocks RAS proteins, which drive tumor growth and were previously considered \"undruggable.\" Approved six months ahead of schedule, Rasonque offers a significant advancement for adults with metastatic pancreatic adenocarcinoma, representing up to 95% of all pancreatic cancer cases, who have received at least one prior treatment. Clinical trials showed patients on Rasonque survived an average of 13.2 months, doubling the 6.7 months seen with standard chemotherapy. While commercial pricing is not yet public, similar oral oncology drugs typically cost $15,000 to $25,000 monthly before insurance. This development marks a pivotal moment in addressing a disease with a historically high mortality rate.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou're all caught up with The Daily Brief. Join us again tomorrow for your next news digest. Thanks for joining us.","scriptTemplateId":2,"articles":[{"title":"Global Visa Interviews On Hold—History Shows A 36% Drop May Follow","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/maryroeloffs/2026/08/26/global-visa-interviews-on-hold-history-shows-a-36-drop-may-follow/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a8eec9e5dbc606308e34e63/960x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=2340\u0026cropY1=49\u0026cropY2=1365","author":"Mary Whitfill Roeloffs","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/7236790","authorUrl":"https://www.forbes.com/sites/maryroeloffs","authorBio":"Mary Roeloffs is a Forbes reporter who covers breaking news with a frequent focus on the entertainment industry, streaming, pop culture, politics, public health and climate change. She joined Forbes in 2023 and lives in Rochester, N.Y. She’s covered Hollywood’s most contentious lawsuits, allegations against Sean “Diddy” Combs, the Trump administration’s controversies, how AI is changing the job market and the 2026 hantavirus outbreak. Roeloffs was included on Editor \u0026 Publisher Magazine’s “25 Under 30” list in 2023 and worked covering local news in the greater Boston area from 2017 to 2023. She graduated with a double major in political science and journalism from Northeastern University. Follow Roeloffs for continued coverage of streaming wars, pop culture news and trending topics.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6487561380bb7d9077f4b945/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=400\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=400","authorSlug":"maryroeloffs","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"The Trump administration has ordered a global halt on all visa applications and canceled scheduled interviews at U.S. embassies, aiming to tighten restrictions and significantly reduce the number of visas issued. This pause is intended for \"in-depth training\" of consular officers, equipping them to reject applicants deemed potential financial burdens on the government. The move follows a judge's recent ruling against the administration's attempt to ban immigration applications from 75 mostly poorer countries, whose citizens were considered likely to need public assistance. Historically, similar pauses have led to substantial drops in visa issuance once processing resumes, disproportionately affecting some nations. The visa action aligns with the administration's broader immigration crackdown, despite data indicating immigrants generally use public safety-net programs at lower rates than native-born Americans. The State Department has not yet announced when visa appointments will resume.","shortHeadline":"Visa Interview Delays","topic":"Visa Policy","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51951-6a8eec0ea676713a6ccc536c"},{"title":"Trump Shares Rant About GOP ‘F- - -ing Up’ As Republicans Criticize His Latest Tariff Moves","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/08/26/trump-shares-rant-about-gop-f---ing-up-as-republicans-criticize-his-latest-tariff-moves/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a8f29a4d8b6dba49270777c/960x0.jpg","author":"Sara Dorn","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/5010031","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn","authorBio":"Sara Dorn is a Forbes news reporter who covers politics, with a focus on elections and Capitol Hill. She joined Forbes in 2022 and is based in New York. She's covered the 2022 midterms, 2024 presidential campaign, the January 6 House committee investigation, former House Speaker Kevin McCarthy's historic ouster, the 2023 State of the Union Address, former President Donald Trump's federal election interference and classified documents cases and his Manhattan hush money case. Dorn graduated in 2012 from the University of Dayton with a degree in journalism. Prior to joining Forbes, she covered New York City and state politics for the New York Post and City and State magazine. Follow her for updates and analysis on the 2024 presidential race, key Senate and House races and developments in Congress and at the White House.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/63457b718c2404f82daa3149/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1284\u0026cropY1=403\u0026cropY2=1687","authorSlug":"saradorn","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"President Donald Trump recently engaged in a wide-ranging social media spree, sharing a post that sharply criticized the Republican Party for \"f---ing up\" and \"sleepwalking toward a midterm reckoning.\" This came amidst significant blowback over his controversial trade policies, including a renewed trade war with Canada and relaxed restrictions on certain beef imports. Trump's online activity, which spanned topics from personal accomplishments and family photos to slamming the Baseball Hall of Fame and criticizing a Republican congressman for endorsing Nikki Haley, coincided with growing concerns about the GOP's prospects in the upcoming midterm elections. His decision to impose 50% tariffs on Canadian goods, prompting retaliation, and to ease beef import rules has angered Republicans in key border and agricultural states. These trade actions, coupled with Trump's consistently low approval ratings—hovering around 33-38% and near a second-term low—and ongoing economic anxieties, are seen as threatening the Republican Party's electoral success. Polls indicate a shift, with more voters now trusting Democrats on economic issues, further complicating the GOP's path to victory.","shortHeadline":"Trump Tariff Criticisms","topic":"Trade Policy","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51554-6a8f2960ee541ab4cbe95ce3"},{"title":"FDA Approves Pancreatic Cancer Drug That Could Double Survival Time","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/maryroeloffs/2026/08/26/fda-approves-pancreatic-cancer-drug-that-could-double-survival-time/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a8f0b6ec3892ca0a62d32b4/960x0.jpg","author":"Mary Whitfill Roeloffs","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/7236790","authorUrl":"https://www.forbes.com/sites/maryroeloffs","authorBio":"Mary Roeloffs is a Forbes reporter who covers breaking news with a frequent focus on the entertainment industry, streaming, pop culture, politics, public health and climate change. She joined Forbes in 2023 and lives in Rochester, N.Y. She’s covered Hollywood’s most contentious lawsuits, allegations against Sean “Diddy” Combs, the Trump administration’s controversies, how AI is changing the job market and the 2026 hantavirus outbreak. Roeloffs was included on Editor \u0026 Publisher Magazine’s “25 Under 30” list in 2023 and worked covering local news in the greater Boston area from 2017 to 2023. She graduated with a double major in political science and journalism from Northeastern University. Follow Roeloffs for continued coverage of streaming wars, pop culture news and trending topics.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6487561380bb7d9077f4b945/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=400\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=400","authorSlug":"maryroeloffs","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"The Food and Drug Administration has approved daraxonrasib, sold as Rasonque by Revolution Medicines, a new once-daily pill for metastatic pancreatic cancer. This first-in-class targeted therapy effectively blocks RAS proteins, which drive tumor growth and were previously considered \"undruggable.\" Approved six months ahead of schedule, Rasonque offers a significant advancement for adults with metastatic pancreatic adenocarcinoma, representing up to 95% of all pancreatic cancer cases, who have received at least one prior treatment. Clinical trials showed patients on Rasonque survived an average of 13.2 months, doubling the 6.7 months seen with standard chemotherapy. While commercial pricing is not yet public, similar oral oncology drugs typically cost $15,000 to $25,000 monthly before insurance. This development marks a pivotal moment in addressing a disease with a historically high mortality rate.","shortHeadline":"Pancreatic Cancer Breakthrough","topic":"Medical Innovation","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51951-6a8f0b4c1c3c9c95087c27a8"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/GhEJNxtw-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"GhEJNxtw","audioDurationS":256,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-08-27T06:30:41-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-08-26-AFTERNOON","cadence":"afternoon","date":"2026-08-26","script":"You're listening to The Daily Brief, your essential news update from Forbes. It's Wednesday, August 26. Today, we're highlighting the key stories covering Innovation, Trade, and a closer look at Politics.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nLeading off with, Big, bold, breaking ground: why eli lilly, amazon and mastercard headline our new most innovative large companies list. Heather Newman has been tracking this development and reports that\n\nForbes has released its \"America's Most Innovative Large Companies\" list, powered by a new data-driven Innovation Index. This index evaluates companies based on patents, research focus, investor confidence, and synthetic audience reactions to company announcements. Mastercard, ranked 10th, is at the forefront of \"agentic commerce,\" where AI assistants autonomously book complex transactions, creating new fraud detection challenges. The company aims to be an \"operating system of economies\" through stablecoins, \"insight tokens,\" and B2B agentic payments. Eli Lilly secured the top spot, recognized for its rapid development of new drugs, including market-leading GLP-1 weight-loss medications like Mounjaro and Zepbound. Its experimental drug, Retatrutide, shows remarkable weight loss in trials, significantly boosting investor confidence. Amazon, at No. 6, demonstrates innovation with its Proteus robots, which autonomously navigate warehouses, adapting to human workers. Boeing scored highly due to its strong innovation narrative and broad patent portfolio. The list underscores how diverse companies are driving America's business engines through R\u0026D and new intellectual property.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nRead the full report on that story at Forbes.com. Moving now to, Canada imposes 50% tariffs on hundreds of U.S. products—doubling steel and aluminum tax, a story that's currently shaping the landscape in Trade.\n\nCanada announced retaliatory tariffs Tuesday, imposing 50% duties on approximately $20 billion worth of U.S. products, mirroring the rates set by President Trump. These taxes, affecting 700 items with rates from 15% to 50%, will take effect on September 8th. The measures include doubling tariffs on steel and aluminum to 50%, alongside duties on dairy, seafood, appliances, furniture, and clothing. To mitigate the impact on its own economy, Canada also unveiled a C$7.5 billion financial support package for businesses and workers. Canadian Finance Minister François-Philippe Champagne stated these \"dollar-for-dollar, rate for rate\" counter-tariffs and support aim to protect its citizens. Meanwhile, President Trump escalated rhetoric, suggesting renaming Lake Ontario \"Lake America\" due to anticipated reduced trade. This trade dispute follows Canada's suspension of talks last Friday, citing \"unfair\" U.S. demands, which triggered Trump's earlier tariffs in July. The U.S. invoked Section 338 of the Tariff Act of 1930, a never-before-used Depression-era law, alleging Canadian discrimination against U.S. goods. Despite the escalation, Canadian Trade Minister Dominic LeBlanc indicated a willingness to reopen discussions, though not passively waiting.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nTo finish today's update, we're turning to, Senate races to watch: talarico leads paxton by 3 points in new texas poll. This headline is generating significant interest within Politics.\n\nRepublicans face a significant risk of losing their narrow Senate majority in the upcoming November midterm elections, with five of the six seats rated as toss-ups by the Cook Political Report currently held by the GOP. Notably, only one of these vulnerable Republican-held seats is in a state that President Donald Trump lost in 2024. In Alaska, Democratic former Representative Mary Peltola is challenging Republican Senator Dan Sullivan, who seeks a third term. Peltola notably outpolled Sullivan by five points in the primary, despite Alaska's strong Republican presidential voting history. Meanwhile, Iowa's open Senate race, replacing retiring Republican Senator Joni Ernst, has shifted to a toss-up between Republican Representative Ashley Hinson and Democratic state Representative Josh Turek. Recent polls show Hinson with a slight lead in a state that has voted Republican in the last three presidential elections. Maine presents another battleground, where Democrat Troy Jackson is running against incumbent Senator Susan Collins. Jackson held a three-point lead over Collins in a July poll, making Maine the sole toss-up state where Trump was defeated in the last presidential election. The race also saw controversy with the withdrawal of a former Democratic nominee due to serious allegations.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nThat wraps up this edition of The Daily Brief. Be sure to check back tomorrow for our next update. Thanks for listening.","scriptTemplateId":1,"articles":[{"title":"Big, Bold, Breaking Ground: Why Eli Lilly, Amazon And Mastercard Headline Our New Most Innovative Large Companies List","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/hnewman/2026/08/25/big-bold-breaking-ground-why-lilly-amazon-and-mastercard-headline-our-new-most-innovative-companies-list/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a8cc14156cf24fd959628df/960x0.jpg?cropX