midterms, 2024 presidential campaign, the January 6 House committee investigation, former House Speaker Kevin McCarthy's historic ouster, the 2023 State of the Union Address, former President Donald Trump's federal election interference and classified documents cases and his Manhattan hush money case. Dorn graduated in 2012 from the University of Dayton with a degree in journalism. Prior to joining Forbes, she covered New York City and state politics for the New York Post and City and State magazine. Follow her for updates and analysis on the 2024 presidential race, key Senate and House races and developments in Congress and at the White House.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/63457b718c2404f82daa3149/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1284\u0026cropY1=403\u0026cropY2=1687","authorSlug":"saradorn","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Republicans face a significant risk of losing their narrow Senate majority in the upcoming November midterm elections, with five of the six seats rated as toss-ups by the Cook Political Report currently held by the GOP. Notably, only one of these vulnerable Republican-held seats is in a state that President Donald Trump lost in 2024. In Alaska, Democratic former Representative Mary Peltola is challenging Republican Senator Dan Sullivan, who seeks a third term. Peltola notably outpolled Sullivan by five points in the primary, despite Alaska's strong Republican presidential voting history. Meanwhile, Iowa's open Senate race, replacing retiring Republican Senator Joni Ernst, has shifted to a toss-up between Republican Representative Ashley Hinson and Democratic state Representative Josh Turek. Recent polls show Hinson with a slight lead in a state that has voted Republican in the last three presidential elections. Maine presents another battleground, where Democrat Troy Jackson is running against incumbent Senator Susan Collins. Jackson held a three-point lead over Collins in a July poll, making Maine the sole toss-up state where Trump was defeated in the last presidential election. The race also saw controversy with the withdrawal of a former Democratic nominee due to serious allegations.","shortHeadline":"Texas Senate Race","topic":"Politics","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51554-6a88a449a4cc22bd7f335442"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/kvR3ZQne-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"kvR3ZQne","audioDurationS":321,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-08-26T10:05:13-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-08-25-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-08-25","script":"Welcome to The Daily Brief, the essential news digest from the Forbes newsroom. It's Tuesday, August 25. Today, we're exploring the stories shaping the global conversation—from the latest in Aviation and Cryptozoology to a deep dive into Horse Racing.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nWe begin with a look at, Why every U.S. airline is going premium. Chris Dong has been following this story and reports that\n\nThe traditional low-cost airline model, featuring single-cabin, no-frills service, is rapidly disappearing in the U.S. as profitability shifts from cheap seats to premium offerings. Travelers' increasing willingness to pay for comfort has driven this change, leading to the demise of carriers like Spirit Airlines. Consequently, first-class sections are physically expanding across the industry, with airlines previously known for their budget focus now investing heavily in premium experiences. JetBlue, a former single-cabin proponent, is launching \"BlueFirst\" domestic first-class seats and \"BlueHouse\" airport lounges, marking a significant strategic reversal. Ultra-low-cost carrier Allegiant is also introducing first-class seating with enhanced amenities and even offering complimentary soft drinks, a departure from its nickel-and-dime philosophy. Southwest Airlines is joining the premium push by planning new airport lounges. Legacy carriers, including American Airlines, are aggressively increasing their premium seat inventory, with American aiming for nearly 40% premium seats on narrowbody planes and reintroducing seatback entertainment. This industry-wide shift, accelerated post-pandemic, is exemplified by Delta Air Lines, where premium revenue growth significantly outpaces main cabin revenue. While this means more premium options for those willing to pay, these additions often reduce economy seating, potentially driving up standard airfares.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou can find the full analysis of that story at Forbes.com. Now, we're turning our attention to, Why explorers are still searching for the abominable snowman, a development that could have a lasting impact on Cryptozoology.\n\nThe Yeti, or Migoi, remains a powerful Himalayan legend, deeply integrated into local culture, especially in Bhutan where Sakteng Wildlife Sanctuary protects its habitat. Early 20th-century explorer sightings and photographs of mysterious tracks sparked global fascination. However, scientific research, including DNA analysis, consistently identifies alleged Yeti remains as known bear species, particularly the ancient Himalayan brown bear. Despite these findings, the legend persists, fueled by the Himalayas' vast, unexplored regions and consistent local eyewitness accounts. Conservationist Daniel C. Taylor emphasizes the Yeti's role in advancing Himalayan conservation through human-wildlife partnerships. Modern expeditions and tourism, like Bhutan's \"Yeti Tracking Trail,\" now leverage the legend for unique cultural and natural explorations. Science, rather than ending the mystery, has revealed the extraordinary history of the real animals behind the enduring Yeti legend. The Yeti thus symbolizes humanity's complex relationship with the wild, blending zoology and cultural identity.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nRounding out today's briefing, we look at, The travers 2026: post positions, odds and golden tempo vs. renegade. This story is currently at the center of the conversation in Horse Racing.\n\nGolden Tempo, the Kentucky Derby and Belmont Stakes champion, has drawn post position No. 9 for the $1.25-million Travers Stakes on August 29. The Triple Crown winner is the narrow 5-2 morning line favorite, with jockey Jose Ortiz aboard. His trainer, Cherie DeVaux, reports Golden Tempo is fit and happy after his historic Belmont win at Saratoga. Renegade, coming off a decisive Jim Dandy victory, is the 3-1 second favorite, starting from post 5. Saratoga oddsmakers surprisingly give Golden Tempo only a 28.57% win chance, just slightly higher than Renegade's 25%. Tyler Gaffalione will ride Renegade, replacing an injured Irad Ortiz Jr. The 10-horse field sets the stage for a potential thrilling stretch duel between these two powerful closers. Jose Ortiz believes Golden Tempo's blinkers have been key to his improvement and likes their outside post. To win, Renegade may need to position closer to the front than usual, exploiting a slight tactical difference from deep-closer Golden Tempo.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou've reached the end of this Daily Brief. Tune in again for tomorrow's updates. Thanks for joining us.","scriptTemplateId":0,"articles":[{"title":"Why Every U.S. Airline Is Going Premium","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/forbestravelguide/2026/08/24/why-every-us-airline-is-going-premium/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a8c41a3160736d67d6bc735/960x0.jpg","author":"Chris Dong","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/7244191","authorUrl":"https://www.forbes.com/sites/forbestravelguide","authorBio":"Chris Dong is Forbes Travel Guide’s aviation correspondent and a seasoned reporter, editorial leader and content strategist with stories for Travel + Leisure, AFAR, The Washington Post and others. His finger is on the pulse of consumer travel, from new hotels and airline partnerships to loyalty programs and destinations, all from a service-oriented lens. Dong also serves as a social video producer for major brands. Whether it’s through editorial, social or other mediums, he engages in innovative storytelling tactics to elevate travel experiences. When not on a plane, Dong calls Los Angeles home after a decade in New York City. You can follow him on Instagram or connect with him on LinkedIn.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/68c443f19f24fea5441b377c/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=749\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=749","authorSlug":"chrisdong","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"The traditional low-cost airline model, featuring single-cabin, no-frills service, is rapidly disappearing in the U.S. as profitability shifts from cheap seats to premium offerings. Travelers' increasing willingness to pay for comfort has driven this change, leading to the demise of carriers like Spirit Airlines. Consequently, first-class sections are physically expanding across the industry, with airlines previously known for their budget focus now investing heavily in premium experiences. JetBlue, a former single-cabin proponent, is launching \"BlueFirst\" domestic first-class seats and \"BlueHouse\" airport lounges, marking a significant strategic reversal. Ultra-low-cost carrier Allegiant is also introducing first-class seating with enhanced amenities and even offering complimentary soft drinks, a departure from its nickel-and-dime philosophy. Southwest Airlines is joining the premium push by planning new airport lounges. Legacy carriers, including American Airlines, are aggressively increasing their premium seat inventory, with American aiming for nearly 40% premium seats on narrowbody planes and reintroducing seatback entertainment. This industry-wide shift, accelerated post-pandemic, is exemplified by Delta Air Lines, where premium revenue growth significantly outpaces main cabin revenue. While this means more premium options for those willing to pay, these additions often reduce economy seating, potentially driving up standard airfares.","shortHeadline":"Airline Premium Services","topic":"Aviation","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/1916-6a8c3e0188c756aaae2e2342"},{"title":"Why Explorers Are Still Searching For The Abominable Snowman","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/jimdobson/2026/08/24/chasing-the-abominable-snowman-inside-the-himalayan-mystery-that-wont-die/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a8d22d5e3f79f8a4c97396b/960x0.jpg","author":"Jim Dobson","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/1302351","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/jimdobson/","authorBio":"Jim Dobson is an award-winning writer, author, and professional raconteur, specializing in exclusive travel, extreme luxury, and high-end destinations.For over 12 years, he has been writing for Forbes, focusing on rare access to extraordinary people, places, and things such as superyachts, underground bunkers, and luxury real estate.An American based in Italy, Dobson has spent over 45 years traveling and has visited more than 90 countries. His work emphasizes immersive, firsthand experiences in remote destinations, often highlighting places that are largely inaccessible to the public.Before focusing on journalism, Dobson built a career in the entertainment industry, working with prominent celebrities and billionaires. This experience informs his access-driven approach and narrative style.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a71fcd9e81e7a6944059bdc/400x0.png?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=800\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=800","authorSlug":"jimdobson","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"The Yeti, or Migoi, remains a powerful Himalayan legend, deeply integrated into local culture, especially in Bhutan where Sakteng Wildlife Sanctuary protects its habitat. Early 20th-century explorer sightings and photographs of mysterious tracks sparked global fascination. However, scientific research, including DNA analysis, consistently identifies alleged Yeti remains as known bear species, particularly the ancient Himalayan brown bear. Despite these findings, the legend persists, fueled by the Himalayas' vast, unexplored regions and consistent local eyewitness accounts. Conservationist Daniel C. Taylor emphasizes the Yeti's role in advancing Himalayan conservation through human-wildlife partnerships. Modern expeditions and tourism, like Bhutan's \"Yeti Tracking Trail,\" now leverage the legend for unique cultural and natural explorations. Science, rather than ending the mystery, has revealed the extraordinary history of the real animals behind the enduring Yeti legend. The Yeti thus symbolizes humanity's complex relationship with the wild, blending zoology and cultural identity.","shortHeadline":"Yeti Search Efforts","topic":"Cryptozoology","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/3307-6a43be351d36e9bb8a26bae7"},{"title":"The Travers 2026: Post Positions, Odds And Golden Tempo Vs. Renegade","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/guymartin/2026/08/24/the-travers-2026-post-positions-odds-and-golden-tempo-vs-renegade/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a6a4ca304102398e2f8ab74/960x0.jpg","author":"Guy Martin","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/1206682","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/guymartin/","authorBio":"Guy Martin has written for Forbes since 2014.  Fluent in German, he is at work on a book for Alfred A. Knopf Publishers about the Cold War in East Berlin. As well, he has reported from London, Warsaw, Prague, Berlin, Paris, Istanbul, Moscow, Beirut, Hong Kong, Lima, Manila, Tel Aviv, St. Moritz, Mindanao, and from his native South for, among others, Esquire, Town and Country, Food \u0026 Wine, Conde Nast Traveler, the Wall Street Journal, the New Yorker, Die Tageszeitung (Berlin), the Observer (London) and other publications in the States and abroad. He was awarded a John J. McCloy Fellowship by the American Council on Germany.","authorAvatar":"https://blogs-images.forbes.com/guymartin/files/2016/05/Guy-Martin_avatar_1463187397-400x400.jpeg","authorSlug":"guymartin","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"Golden Tempo, the Kentucky Derby and Belmont Stakes champion, has drawn post position No. 9 for the $1.25-million Travers Stakes on August 29. The Triple Crown winner is the narrow 5-2 morning line favorite, with jockey Jose Ortiz aboard. His trainer, Cherie DeVaux, reports Golden Tempo is fit and happy after his historic Belmont win at Saratoga. Renegade, coming off a decisive Jim Dandy victory, is the 3-1 second favorite, starting from post 5. Saratoga oddsmakers surprisingly give Golden Tempo only a 28.57% win chance, just slightly higher than Renegade's 25%. Tyler Gaffalione will ride Renegade, replacing an injured Irad Ortiz Jr. The 10-horse field sets the stage for a potential thrilling stretch duel between these two powerful closers. Jose Ortiz believes Golden Tempo's blinkers have been key to his improvement and likes their outside post. To win, Renegade may need to position closer to the front than usual, exploiting a slight tactical difference from deep-closer Golden Tempo.","shortHeadline":"Travers Stakes Outlook","topic":"Horse Racing","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/3133-6a8c4aabec3ebbf8423ae8c4"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/8q7qg4j5-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"8q7qg4j5","audioDurationS":286,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-08-25T06:30:50-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-08-23-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-08-23","script":"Welcome to The Daily Brief, a look at today's top reporting from Forbes. It's Sunday, August 23. On the agenda today: the latest developments in Astronomy, Astronomy, and Travel.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nDiving into the Astronomy space, we start with, Missed the total solar eclipse? now see a ‘blood moon’ across the U.S.. According to reporting from Jamie Carter\n\nThis week, a spectacular partial lunar eclipse will grace the night sky on August 27-28, following the recent total solar eclipse. The full Sturgeon Moon will pass almost entirely through Earth’s darkest shadow, with 96.2% of its surface immersed in the umbra. Visible across North America, South America, Europe, and Africa, this event is poised to be the most impressive lunar eclipse until late 2028. Although not a total \"blood moon,\" the moon will glow a dramatic reddish-orange, with only a thin sliver remaining in direct sunlight. The eclipse will unfold gradually over 5 hours 38 minutes, reaching its maximum at 12:12 a.m. EDT on August 28, or 9:12 p.m. PDT on August 27. Unlike solar eclipses, this celestial show is completely safe to view with the naked eye, requiring no special equipment. Its deep partial nature is uncommon, offering a unique combination of color change and a visible sunlit crescent, along with the sharp edge of Earth's shadow. Around maximum eclipse, the moon's dimness will allow for clearer views of fainter stars and even the Milky Way from dark locations, adding to the experience. This marks a dramatic finale to August's eclipse season.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nFor more context on that story, visit Forbes.com. Next, we shift our focus to, It’s exactly 18 years until the next total solar eclipse in the contiguous U.S., a story that could have significant implications for Astronomy.\n\nWhile Europe recently experienced a total solar eclipse on August 12, the contiguous U.S. only saw a partial event. The next total solar eclipse for the contiguous U.S. is slated for August 22, 2044. This dramatic totality, lasting up to 2 minutes 4 seconds, will sweep across Greenland, northern Canada, and then enter Montana, North Dakota, and South Dakota. Canadian locations like Banff and Jasper National Parks, Calgary, and Edmonton will be in the path, as will U.S. sites such as Glacier National Park. This 2044 eclipse belongs to the Solar Saros 126 family, which generates an eclipse every 18 years. Before that, Alaska will host its own total solar eclipse on March 30, 2033, offering up to 2 minutes 37 seconds of totality in areas like Barrow and Nome, potentially coinciding with northern lights displays. However, the highly anticipated \"Great American Eclipse\" will occur on August 12, 2045, delivering a coast-to-coast totality across the U.S., from California to Florida, with an impressive duration of up to 6 minutes 6 seconds. This 2045 event, alongside the 2044 and 2046 eclipses, exemplifies a recurring pattern of three total eclipses within two years, repeating every 18 years.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nOur final story today looks at, 10 days at the edge of the earth. This remains a major point of conversation in the world of Travel.\n\nAn expedition to Svalbard aboard the MV Freya, a 12-guest ship from Secret Atlas, is an incredible and immersive Arctic experience under the perpetual \"midnight sun.\" The journey provides unique wildlife encounters, including a polar bears, walruses, and seals. Svalbard's 24-hour daylight allows for flexible exploration of its remote landscapes, from glaciers to sea ice. The small ship means a personal experience, allowing guests to observe wildlife closely and responsibly. Guests participate in beach clean-ups, confronting the reality of marine debris and its threat to Arctic species. The expedition underscores that climate change poses a significant threat, by altering the Arctic's very structure. The experience emphasizes that responsible tourism in the High Arctic is not just about witnessing its wonders, but understanding its vulnerabilities and the urgent need for attention to its changing state.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou're all caught up with The Daily Brief. Join us again tomorrow for your next news digest. Thanks for joining us.","scriptTemplateId":2,"articles":[{"title":"Missed The Total Solar Eclipse? Now See A ‘Blood Moon’ Across The U.S.","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/jamiecartereurope/2026/08/23/missed-the-total-solar-eclipse-now-see-a-blood-moon-across-the-us/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a391f0274465c43765840a8/960x0.jpg","author":"Jamie Carter","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/3338193","authorUrl":"https://www.forbes.com/sites/jamiecartereurope","authorBio":"Jamie Carter is an award-winning reporter, author, astrotourism guide, eclipse chaser, aurora hunter and stargazer who covers the night sky and space exploration. The world’s foremost solar eclipse journalist and eclipse, Carter edits WhenIsTheNextEclipse.com and works on eclipse and other astrotourism-themed trips for New Scientist Discovery Tours. The co-author of The Eclipse Effect (2026), Complete Guide to the Total Solar Eclipse 2026 (2025) and Complete Guide to the Total Solar Eclipse 2027 (2026), and author of Chasing the Northern Lights (2026), Stargazing In 2026 and A Stargazing Program for Beginners (2015), he received the 2024 Popular Media Award from the American Astronomical Society’s Solar Physics Division for his coverage of the 2024 total solar eclipse. Writing for Forbes.com since 2018, Carter has also reported on the intense geomagnetic storms and global northern lights in 2024-25, incoming comets, the sun’s solar maximum and the search for intelligent life in the cosmos. He also writes for Space.com, Live Science, Sky \u0026 Telescope, BBC Sky At Night and New Scientist.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/60405466b33286b65965b0ce/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1080\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=1080","authorSlug":"jamiecartereurope","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"This week, a spectacular partial lunar eclipse will grace the night sky on August 27-28, following the recent total solar eclipse. The full Sturgeon Moon will pass almost entirely through Earth’s darkest shadow, with 96.2% of its surface immersed in the umbra. Visible across North America, South America, Europe, and Africa, this event is poised to be the most impressive lunar eclipse un