ted in 2012 from the University of Dayton with a degree in journalism. Prior to joining Forbes, she covered New York City and state politics for the New York Post and City and State magazine. Follow her for updates and analysis on the 2024 presidential race, key Senate and House races and developments in Congress and at the White House.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/63457b718c2404f82daa3149/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1284\u0026cropY1=403\u0026cropY2=1687","authorSlug":"saradorn","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"MyPillow founder Mike Lindell lost the Minnesota GOP gubernatorial primary to House Speaker Lisa Demuth, who secured 43.6% of the vote. Lindell, a prominent promoter of 2020 election fraud claims, finished second with 32.3%. Despite the Associated Press calling the race, Lindell refused to concede, citing unproven \"anomalies.\" His candidacy was notably backed by former President Donald Trump, who praised Lindell's fight for \"free and fair Elections.\" This defeat adds Lindell to a growing list of Trump-endorsed candidates failing in primaries. In the Democratic primary, Senator Amy Klobuchar overwhelmingly won with 89.6%, holding a strong general election lead. Lindell's campaign faced controversies, including residency questions. He also faced a probable campaign law violation for distributing \"Mike Lindell for Governor\" pillows. Furthermore, he recently settled a $1 billion defamation lawsuit with Dominion Voting Systems over his false 2020 election claims.","shortHeadline":"Lindell's Recount Offer","topic":"Elections","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51554-6a7bae147fb951a8ac782060"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/eVv4CjbH-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"eVv4CjbH","audioDurationS":268,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-08-21T06:30:43-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-08-20-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-08-20","script":"Welcome to The Daily Brief, a look at today's top reporting from Forbes. It's Thursday, August 20. On the agenda today: the latest developments in Politics, Legal Affairs, and Real Estate.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nDiving into the Politics space, we start with, ‘I want things to always be right between us,’ natalie harp reportedly wrote to trump. According to reporting from Sara Dorn\n\nTrump aide Natalie Harp reportedly penned a fawning letter to the former president, signing it \"With all my heart, Natalie,\" according to The New York Times. This revelation highlights her extreme devotion, which Senator Jon Ossoff has criticized, accusing Trump of surrounding himself with \"sycophants.\" In the letter, Harp apologized for being an \"embarrassment\" during a Scotland golf outing and described being \"distracted all week,\" forgetting to eat. Her unwavering loyalty has reportedly concerned Trump's inner circle, who worry about her vetting of information due to her constant proximity. Harp's extreme actions include sprinting across a golf course to keep up with Trump's cart and jumping into an SUV trunk to accompany him to a court appearance. Her unique access was further highlighted when she joined Trump on a secret flight from Turkey, while other officials were on a \"decoy\" plane. Despite criticism, White House Press Secretary Karoline Leavitt defended Harp as \"one of the most loyal and hardest working aides.\" Harp, a former OAN anchor, initially gained Trump's attention by crediting him with saving her life through legislation for cancer treatment. Her estranged brother confirmed her long-standing \"obsession\" with presidents, noting she had written similar letters previously.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nFor more context on that story, visit Forbes.com. Next, we shift our focus to, Elon musk renews call for derek chauvin’s release, a story that could have significant implications for Legal Affairs.\n\nBillionaire Elon Musk publicly called for the release of Derek Chauvin, the former Minneapolis police officer convicted of George Floyd's murder, on Wednesday. Musk asserted on X that Chauvin was \"unjustly convicted of murder, therefore he should be freed,\" and retweeted similar sentiments. This statement followed Chauvin's recent petition to vacate his conviction, citing new procedural failures in his trial. Musk's current position marks a significant shift from his earlier, more liberal stance in 2020, when he criticized the lack of charges against other officers involved in Floyd's death. Last year, he had already advocated for a presidential pardon for Chauvin. Prominent right-wing figures, including activist Jack Posobiec and Senator Mike Lee, quickly praised Musk's remarks. Chauvin was found guilty in 2021 and sentenced to 22 1/2 years for Floyd's murder, additionally serving a concurrent 21-year federal sentence for violating Floyd's civil rights. Floyd's death, captured on video showing Chauvin kneeling on his neck, ignited widespread protests against police brutality. Musk's increasing political engagement and support for Republican causes underscore his evolving public persona.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nOur final story today looks at, Cocktails, bomb shelters, very strong stone: trump raves about ballroom as crews race to build it. This remains a major point of conversation in the world of Real Estate.\n\nPresident Donald Trump recently showcased his administration's ambitious White House ballroom project, detailing features like outdoor cocktail space, bomb shelters, and incredibly durable stone rated to last over a million years. During a construction site tour, Trump highlighted the stone's strength, claiming it \"will never break\" and weighs \"35,000 pounds per square inch.\" He emphasized the ballroom's dual purpose, allowing guests to enjoy cocktails outside before entering, while also incorporating a significant \"military component with drones and bomb shelters.\" This rapid construction effort comes as the Supreme Court deliberates whether to halt the project, following lower court rulings that demand congressional approval. The Trump administration argues construction is too advanced to stop, with crews working around the clock to complete it by its 2028 target, seemingly attempting to outpace judicial review. Trump expressed confidence that the conservative-majority court would not rule against the \"beautiful\" design, despite the National Trust for Historic Preservation's legal challenge. The Justice Department also contends that stopping the project, which includes an underground security complex, could harm national security. An appeals court ruling to stop construction is set to take effect Friday without Supreme Court intervention.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou're all caught up with The Daily Brief. Join us again tomorrow for your next news digest. Thanks for joining us.","scriptTemplateId":2,"articles":[{"title":"‘I Want Things To Always Be Right Between Us,’ Natalie Harp Reportedly Wrote To Trump","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/08/19/i-want-things-to-always-be-right-between-us-natalie-harp-reportedly-wrote-to-trump/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a85b3b743b69f77322f6229/960x0.jpg","author":"Sara Dorn","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/5010031","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn","authorBio":"Sara Dorn is a Forbes news reporter who covers politics, with a focus on elections and Capitol Hill. She joined Forbes in 2022 and is based in New York. She's covered the 2022 midterms, 2024 presidential campaign, the January 6 House committee investigation, former House Speaker Kevin McCarthy's historic ouster, the 2023 State of the Union Address, former President Donald Trump's federal election interference and classified documents cases and his Manhattan hush money case. Dorn graduated in 2012 from the University of Dayton with a degree in journalism. Prior to joining Forbes, she covered New York Cit