pt":"You're listening to The Daily Brief, your essential news update from Forbes. It's Wednesday, August 19. Today, we're highlighting the key stories covering Politics, Artificial Intelligence, and a closer look at Politics.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nLeading off with, Jon ossoff blasts trump ‘sycophants’ as he doubles down on natalie harp dig. Sara Dorn has been tracking this development and reports that\n\nSenator Jon Ossoff, D-Ga., doubled down on criticizing Donald Trump, labeling aide Natalie Harp his \"security blanket\" among enabling White House staff. Ossoff explained his rally comments, accusing Trump of neglecting duties to \"travel with Natalie\" and build a ballroom. He justified mentioning Harp due to her frequent public appearances with Trump, raising questions about her influence. Reports describe Harp as a \"human printer\" and \"human binkie,\" curating favorable, unvetted information and typing Trump's social media. Her constant presence was questioned after she accompanied Trump on a secret flight to evade threats. Trump dismissed Ossoff as a \"Pee-wee Herman look-alike,\" avoiding direct comment on Harp. The White House attacked the CNN reporter who questioned Trump about Ossoff's remarks. CNN swiftly defended its journalist against these \"personal attacks.\" Ossoff's viral comments fuel 2028 presidential speculation, though he remains focused on his Georgia Senate re-election.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nRead the full report on that story at Forbes.com. Moving now to, Google’s ‘outrageous’ plan to train AI using spirit airlines’ data blasted by flight attendant union, a story that's currently shaping the landscape in Artificial Intelligence.\n\nGoogle has agreed to pay $10 million for a massive trove of business data from bankrupt Spirit Airlines, raising significant questions about data privacy post-bankruptcy and the burgeoning role of AI. The tech giant outbid other AI firms, including Mercor, to acquire the \"enterprise dataset,\" which Google intends to use for improving its products and AI models. This extensive data includes nearly 100 million passenger names, 13 million active email addresses, 176,000 employee records, and 500 million Microsoft Teams messages. A court-appointed third-party ombudsman will oversee a \"deidentification\" process, implementing \"reasonable measures\" to anonymize the data so it cannot be linked to individuals. Google maintains it will not receive any personal information, focusing on scrubbed, real-world data. However, the Association of Flight Attendants-CWA has filed a court objection, labeling the sale \"outrageous.\"\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nTo finish today's update, we're turning to, Trump calls USS abraham lincoln’s mental health crisis ‘fake report’. This headline is generating significant interest within Politics.\n\nThe USS Abraham Lincoln has been at sea for over 260 days, one of modern history's longest aircraft carrier deployments, sparking a severe mental health crisis among its 5,000 crew. Families and military outlets report multiple suicide attempts; one spouse shared her husband's text, \"he hopes he doesn't wake up tomorrow.\" Beginning November 21 and initially set to end in May, the deployment has been repeatedly extended, forcing the crew into nine months at sea and five months of combat operations against Iran. Congressmen detail inhumane physical conditions: moldy showers, broken facilities, rationed food, and lacking hygiene supplies. Crew had only two restricted days ashore since November. Despite alarming accounts, the Defense Secretary dismissed them as \"fake news.\" A Vice Admiral acknowledged family concerns and mentioned onboard counselors, but prolonged isolation and harsh conditions are taking a significant toll. This extended term far exceeds the modern six-month deployment standard, highlighting Navy pressures.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nThat wraps up this edition of The Daily Brief. Be sure to check back tomorrow for our next update. Thanks for listening.","scriptTemplateId":1,"articles":[{"title":"Jon Ossoff Blasts Trump ‘Sycophants’ As He Doubles Down On Natalie Harp Dig","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/08/18/jon-ossoff-blasts-trump-sycophants-as-he-doubles-down-on-natalie-harp-dig/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a848c263af6e7cfe62c757c/960x0.jpg","author":"Sara Dorn","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/5010031","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn","authorBio":"Sara Dorn is a Forbes news reporter who covers politics, with a focus on elections and Capitol Hill. She joined Forbes in 2022 and is based in New York. She's covered the 2022 midterms, 2024 presidential campaign, the January 6 House committee investigation, former House Speaker Kevin McCarthy's historic ouster, the 2023 State of the Union Address, former President Donald Trump's federal election interference and classified documents cases and his Manhattan hush money case. Dorn graduated in 2012 from the University of Dayton with a degree in journalism. Prior to joining Forbes, she covered New York City and state politics for the New York Post and City and State magazine. Follow her for updates and analysis on the 2024 presidential race, key Senate and House races and developments in Congress and at the White House.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/63457b718c2404f82daa3149/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1284\u0026cropY1=403\u0026cropY2=1687","authorSlug":"saradorn","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Senator Jon Ossoff, D-Ga., doubled down on criticizing Donald Trump, labeling aide Natalie Harp his \"security blanket\" among enabling White House staff. Ossoff explained his rally comments, accusing Trump of neglecting duties to \"travel with Natalie\" and build a ballroom. He justified mentioning Harp due to her frequent public appearances with Trump, raising questions about her