://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/oLHrXRu4-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"oLHrXRu4","audioDurationS":290,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-08-18T06:30:35-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-08-17-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-08-17","script":"Welcome to The Daily Brief, a look at today's top reporting from Forbes. It's Monday, August 17. On the agenda today: the latest developments in Military Health, Entertainment, and AI in Media.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nDiving into the Military Health space, we start with, Admiral claims USS abraham lincoln’s mental health cases are relatively low—despite reported crisis. According to reporting from Mary Whitfill Roeloffs\n\nThe USS Abraham Lincoln has been at sea for over 260 days, one of modern history's longest aircraft carrier deployments, sparking a severe mental health crisis among its 5,000 crew. Families and military outlets report multiple suicide attempts; one spouse shared her husband's text, \"he hopes he doesn't wake up tomorrow.\" Beginning November 21 and initially set to end in May, the deployment has been repeatedly extended, forcing the crew into nine months at sea and five months of combat operations against Iran. Congressmen detail inhumane physical conditions: moldy showers, broken facilities, rationed food, and lacking hygiene supplies. Crew had only two restricted days ashore since November. Despite alarming accounts, the Defense Secretary dismissed them as \"fake news.\" A Vice Admiral acknowledged family concerns and mentioned onboard counselors, but prolonged isolation and harsh conditions are taking a significant toll. This extended term far exceeds the modern six-month deployment standard, highlighting Navy pressures.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nFor more context on that story, visit Forbes.com. Next, we shift our focus to, ‘The end of oak street’—the twist ending, explained, a story that could have significant implications for Entertainment.\n\nDavid Robert Mitchell's *The End Of Oak Street* is an 80s-set sci-fi thriller blending Spielbergian wonder with dark domestic drama. The film follows the Platt family, whose failing marriage mirrors the unraveling reality of their suburban town, Flowervale. Wormholes transport Flowervale to prehistoric times, forcing the Platts to confront their issues while battling scientifically accurate dinosaurs. Greg sacrifices himself to save his family, leading Denise, their children, and an orphaned neighbor, Jeannette, to escape through a wormhole back to 1982. Denise cleverly intervenes, saving her children, warning neighbors, and diverting Greg from the impending anomaly, seemingly creating a happy new timeline. However, the critically acclaimed film's subtle twist reveals a spacetime disruption. The final scene shows two Jeannettes at a barbecue, implying the creation of duplicate individuals and unresolved existential questions. This raises troubling implications about the \"saved\" Greg, who never experienced the original timeline's reconciliation or sacrifice. The bittersweet ending leaves viewers contemplating the profound ripples in reality and the true cost of their survival.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nOur final story today looks at, The anti-ai backlash against ‘supergirl,’ explained. This remains a major point of conversation in the world of AI in Media.\n\nThe film *Supergirl* is embroiled in a new controversy following its streaming debut, as fans identified what appears to be AI-generated concept art in a behind-the-scenes clip. The image, showing Lobo concept art viewed by director Craig Gillespie, exhibited the smooth, blurry characteristics often associated with generative AI. This discovery intensified existing criticism of the film's visuals and its poor box office performance, with many viewing it as evidence of a creative deficit. Online backlash erupted, fueled by concerns over job displacement for human artists and the perceived lack of originality in AI-generated content. Warner Bros. swiftly removed the video, but their silence on the AI accusation was widely interpreted as confirmation by critics. This incident echoes previous AI controversies in media, such as Marvel's *Secret Invasion* and a baffling AI dog podcast promoting *The End of Oak Street*. Even DC Head James Gunn has publicly expressed skepticism about AI, aligning with artists' concerns. The growing \"shame\" around AI use in creative fields highlights a significant reckoning within the industry.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou're all caught up with The Daily Brief. Join us again tomorrow for your next news digest. Thanks for joining us.","scriptTemplateId":2,"articles":[{"title":"Admiral Claims USS Abraham Lincoln’s Mental Health Cases Are Relatively Low—Despite Reported Crisis","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/maryroeloffs/2026/08/16/admiral-claims-uss-abraham-lincolns-mental-health-cases-are-relatively-low-despite-reported-crisis/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a7cb923e9caeb885a59a856/960x0.jpg","author":"Mary Whitfill Roeloffs","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/7236790","authorUrl":"https://www.forbes.com/sites/maryroeloffs","authorBio":"Mary Roeloffs is a Forbes reporter who covers breaking news with a frequent focus on the entertainment industry, streaming, pop culture, politics, public health and climate change. She joined Forbes in 2023 and lives in Rochester, N.Y. She’s covered Hollywood’s most contentious lawsuits, allegations against Sean “Diddy” Combs, the Trump administration’s controversies, how AI is changing the job market and the 2026 hantavirus outbreak. Roeloffs was included on Editor \u0026 Publisher Magazine’s “25 Under 30” list in 2023 and worked covering local news in the greater Boston area from 2017 to 2023. She graduated with a double major in political science and journalism from Northeastern University. Follow Roeloffs for continued coverage of streaming wars, pop culture news and 