founder Mike Lindell lost the Minnesota GOP gubernatorial primary to House Speaker Lisa Demuth, who secured 43.6% of the vote. Lindell, a prominent promoter of 2020 election fraud claims, finished second with 32.3%. Despite the Associated Press calling the race, Lindell refused to concede, citing unproven \"anomalies.\" His candidacy was notably backed by former President Donald Trump, who praised Lindell's fight for \"free and fair Elections.\" This defeat adds Lindell to a growing list of Trump-endorsed candidates failing in primaries. In the Democratic primary, Senator Amy Klobuchar overwhelmingly won with 89.6%, holding a strong general election lead. Lindell's campaign faced controversies, including residency questions. He also faced a probable campaign law violation for distributing \"Mike Lindell for Governor\" pillows. Furthermore, he recently settled a $1 billion defamation lawsuit with Dominion Voting Systems over his false 2020 election claims.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nFor more context on that story, visit Forbes.com. Next, we shift our focus to, Another ship reportedly taking USS abraham lincoln’s place in middle east after reports of mental health crisis, a story that could have significant implications for Military.\n\nThe USS Abraham Lincoln has been at sea for over 260 days, one of modern history's longest aircraft carrier deployments, sparking a severe mental health crisis among its 5,000 crew. Families and military outlets report multiple suicide attempts; one spouse shared her husband's text, \"he hopes he doesn't wake up tomorrow.\" Beginning November 21 and initially set to end in May, the deployment has been repeatedly extended, forcing the crew into nine months at sea and five months of combat operations against Iran. Congressmen detail inhumane physical conditions: moldy showers, broken facilities, rationed food, and lacking hygiene supplies. Crew had only two restricted days ashore since November. Despite alarming accounts, the Defense Secretary dismissed them as \"fake news.\" A Vice Admiral acknowledged family concerns and mentioned onboard counselors, but prolonged isolation and harsh conditions are taking a significant toll. This extended term far exceeds the modern six-month deployment standard, highlighting Navy pressures.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nOur final story today looks at, What to know about josh kushner’s left-leaning politics—amid speculation of trump role in lakers deal. This remains a major point of conversation in the world of Politics.\n\nJosh Kushner, younger brother of Jared Kushner, faces scrutiny over whether his family ties to former President Trump influenced his recent high-profile sports financing ventures, despite his publicly known liberal politics. Kushner, alongside former Disney CEO Bob Iger, recently acquired majority ownership of the Lakers, a deal announced just weeks after his firm, Thrive Capital, saw a planned investment in a FIFA offshoot collapse. While both the White House and Trump deny any involvement in these transactions, the perceived links persist. Kushner has consistently distanced himself politically from Trump, stating his life is guided by liberal values. He notably did not vote for Trump in 2016, attended the Women's March, and has contributed significantly to liberal causes like March for Our Lives and Growth Democrats PAC. His wife, Karlie Kloss, also identifies as a Democrat and has publicly affirmed their differing political views from the Trump family. This contrasts sharply with his brother Jared and father Charles, a close Trump ally who received a presidential pardon and now serves as a U.S. ambassador. The Lakers acquisition, valued at $12.5 billion, marks the largest sports deal ever, occurring amidst a federal probe into the seller, Mark Walter's financial firm, and resistance to private equity in the FIFA deal.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou're all caught up with The Daily Brief. Join us again tomo