ing \"Mike Lindell for Governor\" pillows. Furthermore, he recently settled a $1 billion defamation lawsuit with Dominion Voting Systems over his false 2020 election claims.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nRead the full report on that story at Forbes.com. Moving now to, Eclipse 2026 headache: what to do if you looked at the sun and your eyes hurt, a story that's currently shaping the landscape in Health.\n\nFollowing the April 8, 2024, total solar eclipse, Google searches for \"eyes hurt\" surged, particularly in areas along the path of totality, indicating many viewers may have lacked proper eye protection. This trend highlights the delayed onset of solar retinopathy symptoms, which can manifest hours or even days after sun exposure. As a partial solar eclipse approaches on August 12, visible across Alaska, 26 U.S. states, and Canada, experts reiterate crucial safety warnings. Viewers must use ISO 12312-2-certified eclipse glasses at all times for any partial eclipse, as ordinary sunglasses offer no protection. Binoculars, cameras, and telescopes require secure solar filters. Solar retinopathy, a painless retinal damage, can cause blurred vision, blind spots, or light sensitivity, and requires immediate medical attention from an ophthalmologist, as there is no proven treatment. While North America experiences a partial eclipse, a total solar eclipse will be visible in eastern Greenland, western Iceland, and northern Spain. Only within this path of totality is it safe to briefly remove eye protection to view the sun's corona. Confusion about this safety rule is common, underscoring the importance of adhering to guidelines to prevent permanent eye damage.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nTo finish today's update, we're turning to, Why polls got wisconsin governor’s race wrong—after socialist francesca hong loses. This headline is generating significant interest within Politics.\n\nSocialist Francesca Hong, once heavily favored to win the Wisconsin Democratic gubernatorial primary, ultimately lost to Milwaukee County Executive Crowley by a narrow 0.4 percentage points. Betting odds had Hong at 95.5% to win on Monday, though this significantly narrowed to 58% by Tuesday afternoon. Pre-election polls proved unreliable due to several last-minute developments. A Marquette Law poll from late July showed Hong leading with 38%, followed by Lt. Gov. Mandela Barnes at 16% and Crowley at 7%. However, this was taken before Barnes dropped out and shortly after Crowley re-entered the race. Earlier polls also featured high undecided voter percentages and small sample sizes. Hong's controversial 2021 social media posts about canceling Thanksgiving, which gained national attention in early August, further complicated the race dynamics. While a later State Navigate poll showed Hong with 44% to Crowley's 22%, it still underestimated Crowley's eventual support. Crowley secured the endorsement of retiring Governor Tony Evers, while Hong garnered support from some progressive figures like Reps. Ro Khanna and Ilhan Omar, though not from others such as Sen. Bernie Sanders or Rep. Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez. The presence of other candidates like Kelda Roys and Joel Brennan, who collectively took over 12% of the vote, also impacted the final outcome.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nThat wraps up this edition of The Daily Brief. Be sure to check back tomorrow for our next update. Thanks for listening.","scriptTemplateId":1,"articles":[{"title":"MyPillow Founder Mike Lindell Won’t Concede Losing Minnesota Republican Gubernatorial Primary","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/08/12/mypillow-founder-mike-lindell-wont-concede-losing-minnesota-republican-gubernatorial-primary/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a7bfa85542e3bc7a6d30422/960x0.jpg","author":"Sara Dorn","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/5010031","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn","authorBio":"Sara Dorn is a Forbes