on claims.","shortHeadline":"Lindell's Non-Concession","topic":"Politics","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51554-6a7bae147fb951a8ac782060"},{"title":"Eclipse 2026 Headache: What To Do If You Looked At The Sun And Your Eyes Hurt","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/jamiecartereurope/2026/08/12/eclipse-2026-headache-what-to-do-if-you-looked-at-the-sun-and-your-eyes-hurt/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a709f99a909ad833a0b79d8/960x0.jpg?cropX1=12\u0026cropX2=4554\u0026cropY1=325\u0026cropY2=2878","author":"Jamie Carter","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/3338193","authorUrl":"https://www.forbes.com/sites/jamiecartereurope","authorBio":"Jamie Carter is an award-winning reporter, author, astrotourism guide, eclipse chaser, aurora hunter and stargazer who covers the night sky and space exploration. The world’s foremost solar eclipse journalist and eclipse, Carter edits WhenIsTheNextEclipse.com and works on eclipse and other astrotourism-themed trips for New Scientist Discovery Tours. The co-author of The Eclipse Effect (2026), Complete Guide to the Total Solar Eclipse 2026 (2025) and Complete Guide to the Total Solar Eclipse 2027 (2026), and author of Chasing the Northern Lights (2026), Stargazing In 2026 and A Stargazing Program for Beginners (2015), he received the 2024 Popular Media Award from the American Astronomical Society’s Solar Physics Division for his coverage of the 2024 total solar eclipse. Writing for Forbes.com since 2018, Carter has also reported on the intense geomagnetic storms and global northern lights in 2024-25, incoming comets, the sun’s solar maximum and the search for intelligent life in the cosmos. He also writes for Space.com, Live Science, Sky \u0026 Telescope, BBC Sky At Night and New Scientist.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/60405466b33286b65965b0ce/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1080\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=1080","authorSlug":"jamiecartereurope","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"Following the April 8, 2024, total solar eclipse, Google searches for \"eyes hurt\" surged, particularly in areas along the path of totality, indicating many viewers may have lacked proper eye protection. This trend highlights the delayed onset of solar retinopathy symptoms, which can manifest hours or even days after sun exposure. As a partial solar eclipse approaches on August 12, visible across Alaska, 26 U.S. states, and Canada, experts reiterate crucial safety warnings. Viewers must use ISO 12312-2-certified eclipse glasses at all times for any partial eclipse, as ordinary sunglasses offer no protection. Binoculars, cameras, and telescopes require secure solar filters. Solar retinopathy, a painless retinal damage, can cause blurred vision, blind spots, or light sensitivity, and requires immediate medical attention from an ophthalmologist, as there is no proven treatment. While North America experiences a partial eclipse, a total solar eclipse will be visible in eastern Greenland, western Iceland, and northern Spain. Only within this path of totality is it safe to briefly remove eye protection to view the sun's corona. Confusion about this safety rule is common, underscoring the importance of adhering to guidelines to prevent permanent eye damage.","shortHeadline":"Eclipse Eye Damage","topic":"Health","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/6117-6a709ef8b905055dc9881382"},{"title":"Why Polls Got Wisconsin Governor’s Race Wrong—After Socialist Francesca Hong Loses","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/08/12/why-polls-got-wisconsin-governors-race-wrong-after-socialist-francesca-hong-loses/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a7c77844010efdc5a65bd04/960x0.jpg","author":"Sara Dorn","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/5010031","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn","authorBio":"Sara Dorn is a Forbes news reporter who covers politics, with a focus on elections and Capitol Hill. She joined Forbes in 2022 and is based in New York. She's covered the 2022 midterms, 2024 presidential campaign, the January 6 House committee investigation, former House Speaker Kevin McCarthy's historic ouster, the 2023 State of the Union Address, former President Donald Trump's federal election interference and classified documents cases and his Manhattan hush money case. Dorn graduated in 2012 from the University of Dayton with a degree in journalism. Prior to joining Forbes, she covered New York City and state politics for the New York Post and City and State magazine. Follow her for updates and analysis on the 2024 presidential race, key Senate and House races and developments in Congress and at the White House.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/63457b718c2404f82daa3149/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1284\u0026cropY1=403\u0026cropY2=1687","authorSlug":"saradorn","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Socialist Francesca Hong, once heavily favored to win the Wisconsin Democratic gubernatorial primary, ultimately lost to Milwaukee County Executive Crowley by a narrow 0.4 percentage points. Betting odds had Hong at 95.5% to win on Monday, though this significantly narrowed to 58% by Tuesday afternoon. Pre-election polls proved unreliable due to several last-minute developments. A Marquette Law poll from late July showed Hong leading with 38%, followed by Lt. Gov. Mandela Barnes at 16% and Crowley at 7%. However, this was taken before Barnes dropped out and shortly after Crowley re-entered the race. Earlier polls also featured high undecided voter percentages and small sample sizes. Hong's controversial 2021 social media posts about canceling Thanksgiving, which gained national attention in early August, further complicated the race dynamics. While a later State Navigate poll showed Hong with 44% to Crowley's 22%, it still underestimated Crowley's eventual support. Crowley secured the endorsement of retiring Governor Tony Evers, while Hong garnered support from some progressive figures like Reps. Ro Khanna and Ilhan Omar, though not from others such as Sen. Bernie Sanders or Rep. Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez. The presence of other candidates like Kelda Roys and Joel Brennan, who collectively took over 12% of the vote, also impacted the final outcome.","shortHeadline":"Polling Inaccuracies","topic":"Politics","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51554-6a7c7746d0b1f0727f25c046"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/TgtN8KcZ-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"TgtN8KcZ","audioDurationS":296,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-08-13T06:30:35-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-08-12-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-08-12","script":"Welcome to The Daily Brief, the essential news digest from the Forbes newsroom. It's Wednesday, August 12. Today, we're exploring the stories shaping the global conversation—from the latest in Politics and Tourism to a deep dive into Ecotourism.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nWe begin with a look at, How left-wing twitch streamer hasan piker has divided democrats. Sara Dorn has been following this story and reports that\n\nLeft-wing Twitch streamer Hasan Piker has become a central figure in the Democratic Party's internal struggle over the progressive movement, with his incendiary politics drawing fire from both conservatives and centrist Democrats. A recent Rolling Stone profile reignited debate, prompting conservatives like Alyssa Farrah Griffin and Elon Musk to criticize Piker and, by extension, the Democratic Party. Centrist Democrats, including Joe Scarborough, have urged progressives to distance themselves, citing Piker's controversial anti-Israel rhetoric and past comments, such as his 2019 statement that \"America deserved 9/11.\" Michigan Democratic Senate nominee Abdul El-Sayed faced intense backlash for campaigning with Piker, with figures like Sen. John Fetterman and strategist James Carville condemning the association. While El-Sayed partially distanced himself from Piker's \"dumb statement\" on 9/11, he defended appearing with him, arguing that association doesn't imply full agreement. Rep. Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez, who along with other progressives like Rashida Tlaib and Ilhan Omar has appeared with Piker, emphasized the need for Democrats to engage young voters online. Piker, known for his staunch anti-Israel views and often hyperbolic rhetoric, has characterized some of his controversial statements as performance. The ongoing debate highlights concerns within the Democratic Party about the electability of progressive candidates, many of whom Piker has supported, in upcoming general elections.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou can find the full analysis of that story at Forbes.com. Now, we're turning our attention to, Canadian visitors to U.S. were up 10% in july—but new steep tariffs threaten tourism lift, a development that could have a lasting impact on Tourism.\n\nCanadian visitor numbers to the U.S. rose 10% in July compared to last year, marking the fourth consecutive month of improvement. This increase, primarily driven by a 13% rise in car trips, is largely attributed to a \"base-year effect\" following exceptionally low visitation in 2025. Despite this uptick, overall Canadian travel to the U.S. remains down 29% compared to July 2024, with air travel also slightly down. Tourism leaders, however, caution that the Trump administration's new round of 50% punitive tariffs, set to take effect on August 19, could reverse this nascent recovery.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nRounding out today's briefing, we look at, Wolf populations are soaring across europe, and so is wildlife tourism. This story is currently at the center of the conversation in Ecotourism.\n\nWolves are making an astonishing comeback across Europe, with populations surging by 58% to over 21,500 individuals by 2022, a testament to decades of legal protection and ecological recovery. This remarkable return, often in areas previously devoid of large carnivores, has transformed the wolf from a historical adversary into a symbol of rewilding and a magnet for ecotourism. Specialized outfitters now offer unique experiences, from tracking packs in Italy and Sweden to overnight photography in Finnish hides, catering to a growing desire to connect with wild nature. These responsible tours, emphasizing the thrill of potential sightings over guarantees, are crucial for local economies. They inject vital revenue into rural communities, supporting guides and accommodations, and fostering a \"virtuous cycle\" where wildlife presence drives economic growth, as seen in Italy's Abruzzo region. While recent policy changes may allow for more flexible wolf management, the species' resurgence highlights its resilience and its new role in supporting sustainable green economies across the continent.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou've reached the end of this Daily Brief. Tune in again for tomorrow's updates. Thanks for joining us.","scriptTemplateId":0,"articles":[{"title":"How Left-Wing Twitch Streamer Hasan Piker Has Divided Democrats","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/08/11/how-left-wing-twitch-streamer-hasan-piker-has-divided-democrats/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a7b2fdb6716f540e84c15ac/960x0.jpg","author":"Sara Dorn","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/5010031","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn","authorBio":"Sara Dorn is a Forbes news reporter who covers politics, with a focus on elections and Capitol Hill. She joined Forbes in 2022 and is based in New York. She's covered the 2022 midterms, 2024 presidential campaign, the January 6 House committee investigation, former House Speaker Kevin McCarthy's historic ouster, the 2023 State of the Union Address, former President Donald Trump's federal election interference and classified documents cases and his Manhattan hush money case. Dorn graduated in 2012 from the University of Dayton w