u0026cropY1=6\u0026cropY2=927","author":"Jim Dobson","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/1302351","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/jimdobson/","authorBio":"Jim Dobson is an award-winning writer, author, and professional raconteur, specializing in exclusive travel, extreme luxury, and high-end destinations.For over 12 years, he has been writing for Forbes, focusing on rare access to extraordinary people, places, and things such as superyachts, underground bunkers, and luxury real estate.An American based in Italy, Dobson has spent over 45 years traveling and has visited more than 90 countries. His work emphasizes immersive, firsthand experiences in remote destinations, often highlighting places that are largely inaccessible to the public.Before focusing on journalism, Dobson built a career in the entertainment industry, working with prominent celebrities and billionaires. This experience informs his access-driven approach and narrative style.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a71fcd9e81e7a6944059bdc/400x0.png?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=800\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=800","authorSlug":"jimdobson","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"Wolves are making an astonishing comeback across Europe, with populations surging by 58% to over 21,500 individuals by 2022, a testament to decades of legal protection and ecological recovery. This remarkable return, often in areas previously devoid of large carnivores, has transformed the wolf from a historical adversary into a symbol of rewilding and a magnet for ecotourism. Specialized outfitters now offer unique experiences, from tracking packs in Italy and Sweden to overnight photography in Finnish hides, catering to a growing desire to connect with wild nature. These responsible tours, emphasizing the thrill of potential sightings over guarantees, are crucial for local economies. They inject vital revenue into rural communities, supporting guides and accommodations, and fostering a \"virtuous cycle\" where wildlife presence drives economic growth, as seen in Italy's Abruzzo region. While recent policy changes may allow for more flexible wolf management, the species' resurgence highlights its resilience and its new role in supporting sustainable green economies across the continent.","shortHeadline":"Wildlife Tourism Boom","topic":"Ecotourism","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/3307-6a6f357d36f53e82b37ee2a1"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/gKLRq7U7-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"gKLRq7U7","audioDurationS":272,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-08-12T06:30:43-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-08-11-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-08-11","script":"Welcome to The Daily Brief, a look at today's top reporting from Forbes. It's Tuesday, August 11. On the agenda today: the latest developments in Corporate Earnings, Political Strategy, and Political Candidates.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nDiving into the Corporate Earnings space, we start with, Geico earnings plummet 45% in hit to berkshire hathaway's insurance business. According to reporting from Mary Whitfill Roeloffs\n\nBerkshire Hathaway's Geico reported a nearly 45% drop in Q2 2026 pre-tax underwriting earnings, falling to $994 million. This significant decline, impacting its most profitable sector, stemmed from increased auto claims and sharply rising bodily injury costs. Geico's loss ratio climbed to 76.6% in Q2 and 75.3% for the first half, up nearly five percentage points. Bodily injury claims rose 5% in frequency and 10% in average cost during H1 2026, worsening from 2025 and reflecting a trend where injury payouts now exceed physical damage claims. The surge stems from social factors like higher medical costs, affordability issues, and aggressive legal strategies, not accident frequency; attorney involvement and rising healthcare expenses inflate costs. ADAS has also reduced minor collisions, shifting the claims pool disproportionately towards severe, injury-prone crashes. This operational deterioration is notable given Geico's recent recovery, despite Berkshire Hathaway's robust financial cushion.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nFor more context on that story, visit Forbes.com. Next, we shift our focus to, Why republicans might be rooting for socialist francesca hong in wisconsin primary, a story that could have significant implications for Political Strategy.\n\nDemocratic socialist State Rep. Francesca Hong leads Wisconsin's gubernatorial primary polls, raising alarms among some Democrats about her general election viability against a Republican. This concern reflects a national trend where progressive primary winners face electability doubts. Surveys show Hong with a significant lead, though many voters remain undecided. Incumbent Gov. Tony Evers, who endorsed opponent David Crowley, openly questioned Hong's ability to defeat presumptive Republican nominee Tom Tiffany and wouldn't commit to supporting her. Crowley also stated, \"people don't like socialists,\" believing Hong \"can't win.\" Polling suggests Hong could lose support among primary voters to Tiffany in November, with an earlier Marquette poll showing her trailing him. Republicans, viewing Hong as a weaker candidate, are already running ads through groups like Right Direction Wisconsin, branding her too liberal and highlighting her call to abolish ICE. This strategy is seen by some as an attempt to boost Hong's primary chances, aiming for a more beatable general election opponent.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nOur final story today looks at, Darline graham’s riding with trump—but political views beyond that still unclear as primary looms. This remains a major point of conversation in the world of Political Candidates.\n\nSen. Darline Graham Nordone, R-S.C., the younger sister of the late Sen. Lindsey Graham, announced her bid to permanently succeed her brother, whom she was appointed to replace temporarily. Nordone, who served as commissioner for the South Carolina Commission for the Blind since 2019, has a background in disability rights advocacy, including helping to end subminimum wage policy in the state. Colleagues describe her as not overtly political, with her only recorded political donation being $100. She was raised by her brother after their parents' deaths when she was 13 and often campaigned with him. Nordone expressed confidence in her ability to serve, stating she learned hard work from Lindsey. Her candidacy comes with an endorsement from former President Donald Trump, who called her a \"spectacular person.\" She will compete in a special primary on August 11 against Republican Reps. Russell Fry and Ralph Norman, among others, with the winner facing Democrat Annie Andrews in November. Lindsey Graham, a four-term senator and foreign policy hawk, died on July 11.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou're all caught up with The Daily Brief. Join us again tomorrow for your next news digest. Thanks for joining us.","scriptTemplateId":2,"articles":[{"title":"Geico Earnings Plummet 45% In Hit To Berkshire Hathaway's Insurance Business","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/maryroeloffs/2026/08/10/geico-earnings-plummet-45-in-hit-to-berkshire-hathaways-insurance-business/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a79ebfcc7e46bf932eaae55/960x0.jpg","author":"Mary Whitfill Roeloffs","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/7236790","authorUrl":"https://www.forbes.com/sites/maryroeloffs","authorBio":"Mary Roeloffs is a Forbes reporter who covers breaking news with a frequent focus on the entertainment industry, streaming, pop culture, politics, public health and climate change. She joined Forbes in 2023 and lives in Rochester, N.Y. She’s covered Hollywood’s most contentious lawsuits, allegations against Sean “Diddy” Combs, the Trump administration’s controversies, how AI is changing the job market and the 2026 hantavirus outbreak. Roeloffs was included on Editor \u0026 Publisher Magazine’s “25 Under 30” list in 2023 and worked covering local news in the greater Boston area from 2017 to 2023. She graduated with a double major in political science and journalism from Northeastern University. Follow Roeloffs for continued coverage of streaming wars, pop culture news and trending topics.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6487561380bb7d9077f4b945/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=400\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=400","authorSlug":"maryroeloffs","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Berkshire Hathaway's Geico reported a nearly 45% drop in Q2 2026 pre-tax underwriting earnings, falling to $994 million. This significant decline, impacting its most profitable sector, stemmed from increased auto claims and sharply rising bodily injury costs. Geico's loss ratio climbed to 76.6% in Q2 and 75.3% for the first half, up nearly five percentage points. Bodily injury claims rose 5% in frequency and 10% in average cost during H1 2026, worsening from 2025 and reflecting a trend where injury payouts now exceed physical damage claims. The surge stems from social factors like higher medical costs, affordability issues, and aggressive legal strategies, not accident frequency; attorney involvement and rising healthcare expenses inflate costs. ADAS has also reduced minor collisions, shifting the claims pool disproportionately towards severe, injury-prone crashes. This operational deterioration is notable given Geico's recent recovery, despite Berkshire Hathaway's robust financial cushion.","shortHeadline":"Insurance Business Woes","topic":"Corporate Earnings","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51951-6a79eb40e6e04d246ecbda91"},{"title":"Why Republicans Might Be Rooting For Socialist Francesca Hong In Wisconsin Primary","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/08/10/why-republicans-might-be-rooting-for-socialist-francesca-hong-in-wisconsin-primary/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a79f074f946de665d0a6e25/960x0.jpg","author":"Sara Dorn","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/5010031","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn","authorBio":"Sara Dorn is a Forbes news reporter who covers politics, with a focus on elections and Capitol Hill. She joined Forbes in 2022 and is based in New York. She's covered the 2022 midterms, 2024 presidential campaign, the January 6 House committee investigation, former House Speaker Kevin McCarthy's historic ouster, the 2023 State of the Union Address, former President Donald Trump's federal election interference and classified documents cases and his Manhattan hush money case. Dorn graduated in 2012 from the University of Dayton with a degree in journalism. Prior to joining Forbes, she covered New York City and state politics for the New York Post and City and State magazine. Follow her for updates and analysis on the 2024 presidential race, key Senate and House races and developments in Congress and at the White House.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/63457b718c2404f82daa3149/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1284\u0026cropY1=403\u0026cropY2=1687","authorSlug":"saradorn","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Democratic socialist State Rep. Francesca Hong leads Wisconsin's gubernatorial primary polls, raising alarms among some Democrats about her general election viability against a Republican. This concern reflects a national trend where progressive primary winners face electability doubts. Surveys show Hong with a significant lead, though many voters remain undecided. Incumbent Gov. Tony Evers, who endorsed opponent David Crowley, openly questioned Hong's ability to defeat presumptive Republican nominee Tom Tiffany and wouldn't commit to supporting her. Crowley also stated, \"people don't like socialists,\" believing Hong \"can't win.\" Polling suggests Hong could lose support among primary voters to Tiffany in November, with an earlier Marquette poll showing her trailing him. Republicans, viewing Hong as a weaker candidate, are already running ads through groups like Right Direction Wisconsin, branding her too liberal and highlighting her call to abolish ICE. This strategy is seen by some as an attempt to boost Hong's primary chances, aiming for a more beatable general election opponent.","shortHeadline":"Primary Election Dynamics","topic":"Political Strategy","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51554-6a79f03e008547865a71c6c3"},{"title":"Darline Graham’s Riding With Trump—But Political Views Beyond That Still Unclear As Primary Looms","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/08/10/darline-grahams-riding-with-trump-but-political-views-beyond-that-still-unclear-as-primary-looms/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a5e9e3e7c769d99ff3add62/960x0.jpg","author":"Sara Dorn","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/5010031","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn","authorBio":"Sara Dorn is a Forbes news reporter who covers politics, with a focus on elections and Capitol Hill. She joined Forbes in 2022 and is based in New York. She's covered the 2022 midterms, 2024 presidential campaign, the January 6 House committee investigation, former House Speaker Kevin McCarthy's historic ouster, the 2023 State of the Union Address, former President Donald Trump's federal election interference and classified documents cases and his Manhattan hush money case. Dorn graduated in 2012 from the University of Dayton with a degree in journalism. Prior to joining Forbes, she covered New York City and state politics for the New York Post and City and State magazine. Follow her for updates and analysis on the 2024 presidential race, key Senate and House races and developments in Congress and at the White House.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/63457b718c2404f82daa3149/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1284\u0026cropY1=403\u0026cropY2=1687","authorSlug":"saradorn","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Sen. Darline Graham Nordone, R-S.C., the younger sister of the late Sen. Lindsey Graham, announced her bid to permanently succeed her brother, whom she was appointed to replace temporarily. Nordone, who served as commissioner for the South Carolina Commission for the Blind since 2019, has a background in disability rights advocacy, including helping to end subminimum wage policy in the state. Colleagues describe her as not overtly political, with her only recorded political donation being $100. She was raised by her brother after their parents' deaths when she was 13 and often campaigned with him. Nordone expressed confidence in her ability to serve, stating she learned hard work from Lindsey. Her candidacy comes with an endorsement from former President Donald Trump, who called her a \"spectacular person.\" She will compete in a special primary on August 11 against Republican Reps. Russell Fry and Ralph Norman, among others, with the winner facing Democrat Annie Andrews in November. Lindsey Graham, a four-term senator and foreign policy hawk, died on July 11.","shortHeadline":"Candidate's Political Views","topic":"Political Candidates","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51554-6a5e9e0dd52c2c3659456539"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/LEeOtn8T-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"LEeOtn8T","audioDurationS":273,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-08-11T06:30:44-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-08-10-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-08-10","script":"You're listening to The Daily Brief, your essential news update from Forbes. It's Monday, August 10. Today, we're highlighting the key stories covering Dining, Public Health, and a closer look at Entrepreneurship.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nLeading off with, 7 new london restaurants to try, summer 2026. Joanne Shurvell has been tracking this development and reports that\n\nLondon's summer 2026 culinary landscape is vibrant with ambitious new openings, spanning diverse international cuisines. Mayfair's Mimi Mei Fair offers contemporary Thai, serving spicy classics and fire-led dishes like Peking Duck Penang Curry in a Georgian setting. In Soho, Miokuru provides an intimate 20-seat chef's counter, refining British seafood with Japanese techniques, featuring exquisite crab hand rolls and seared trout sashimi. The OWO has welcomed Langosteria, Milan's renowned seafood institution, bringing glamorous Italian dining with expertly grilled fish and signature King Crab. Borough's Oudh 1722, from Michelin-starred Aktar Islam, introduces royal Awadhi cuisine, featuring street-food snacks, kebabs, and slow-cooked dishes like oxtail biryani. MA/NA, in Mayfair, presents a Japanese menu with elegant sushi, robata-grilled items, and inventive small plates. Cafe Clement offers stylish all-day French and Italian dining, led by former River Café chef Danny Bohan. Latine, also in Mayfair, blends classic French techniques with vibrant Latin American flavors, featuring Boeuf Bourguignon Tacos, a dedicated Crudo Bar, and unique desserts.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nRead the full report on that story at Forbes.com. Moving now to, Microplastics are part of everyday life. what does that mean for our health?, a story that's currently shaping the landscape in Public Health.\n\nPlastic is everywhere: in our buildings, clothing, food, cleaning and personal care products, and countless things we use every day. Scientists are now finding microscopic plastic particles throughout the human body, including the brain. What that means for our health is a much bigger question, and one researchers are only beginning to answer.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nTo finish today's update, we're turning to, How azature pogosian bet on anti-scale strategy and won. This headline is generating significant interest within Entrepreneurship.\n\nThe global luxury jewelry market is rapidly expanding, projected to exceed $580 billion by decade's end, increasingly leveraging digital strategies and accessibility. Yet, Los Angeles jeweler Azature Pogosian, dubbed the \"Black Diamond King,\" built his brand on a contrasting philosophy. For nearly 25 years, he has prioritized deep relationships, bespoke craftsmanship, and organic word-of-mouth over mass reach and extensive ad spend. His accidental journey began with a $5,000 investment from his uncle, steering him from fashion into jewelry. Pogosian rose to prominence by transforming overlooked black diamonds into highly coveted pieces, notably gaining traction when Rihanna wore his designs. Pogosian defines true luxury by craftsmanship, the time invested, and the personal meaning of an \"eternal heirloom,\" not merely its cost. He consistently rejects mass distribution, asserting fine jewelry is too personal for off-the-shelf sales. This distinctive approach allows him to thrive amidst a diamond market increasingly shaped by lab-grown stones, emphasizing lasting value and legacy.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nThat wraps up this edition of The Daily Brief. Be sure to check back tomorrow for our next update. Thanks for listening.","scriptTemplateId":1,"articles":[{"title":"7 New London Restaurants To Try, Summer 2026","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/joanneshurvell/2026/08/09/7-new-london-restaurants-to-try-summer-2026/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a70a1ae05d35267f61d7a8b/960x0.jpg","author":"Joanne Shurvell","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/1986577","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/joanneshurvell/","authorBio":"London-based writer Joanne Shurvell covers travel, food, culture and fashion for a variety of publications, including Forbes since 2016. She co-founded PayneShurvell, a contemporary art gallery in London and was co-author of the YouTube art series, Great Art Cities Explained with James Payne. She is co-author of the Citysketch series of books that includes London, Paris and New York and the author of Fantastic Forgeries: Paint Like Van Gogh. Her photographer partner Paul Allen often supplies photos for her features. Follow them on Instagram at @joshurvell and @andfotography2.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/5f2bdc26cfbeb32eeac91e7e/400x0.jpg?cropX1=287\u0026cropX2=665\u0026cropY1=170\u0026cropY2=548","authorSlug":"joanneshurvell","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"London's summer 2026 culinary landscape is vibrant with ambitious new openings, spanning diverse international cuisines. Mayfair's Mimi Mei Fair offers contemporary Thai, serving spicy classics and fire-led dishes like Peking Duck Penang Curry in a Georgian setting. In Soho, Miokuru provides an intimate 20-seat chef's counter, refining British seafood with Japanese techniques, featuring exquisite crab hand rolls and seared trout sashimi. The OWO has welcomed Langosteria, Milan's renowned seafood institution, bringing glamorous Italian dining with expertly grilled fish and signature King Crab. Borough's Oudh 1722, from Michelin-starred Aktar Islam, introduces royal Awadhi cuisine, featuring street-food snacks, kebabs, and slow-cooked dishes like oxtail biryani. MA/NA, in Mayfair, presents a Japanese menu with elegant sushi, robata-grilled items, and inventive small plates. Cafe Clement offers stylish all-day French and Italian dining, led by former River Café chef Danny Bohan. Latine, also in Mayfair, blends classic French techniques with vibrant Latin American flavors, featuring Boeuf Bourguignon Tacos, a dedicated Crudo Bar, and unique desserts.","shortHeadline":"New London Restaurants","topic":"Dining","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/4302-6a635dde5d2e9eda9d6a10f1"},{"title":"Microplastics Are Part Of Everyday Life. What Does That Mean For Our Health?","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/meggenharris/2026/08/09/microplastics-are-part-of-everyday-life-what-does-that-mean-for-our-health/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a7908fabae80ee30e78ab2f/960x0.jpg","author":"Meggen Harris","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/1968475","authorUrl":"https://www.forbes.com/sites/meggenharris","authorBio":"Meggen Harris is a respected journalist with nearly a decade of experience chronicling the stories behind successful entrepreneurs and innovative brands. Her coverage spans a diverse range of lifestyle topics, with a particular focus on beauty, fashion, travel, and wellbeing. Harris is known for her willingness to share her personal experiences. She has openly written about her journey of treating depression and anxiety with clinician-guided ketamine therapy—a pioneering approach to treating mental health challenges with psychedelics. She has also provided candid accounts of her adventures exploring the latest non-invasive aesthetic treatments and her experiences at some of the world's most notable longevity clinics. Harris's impressive roster of interviewees includes renowned CEOs, celebrity-entrepreneurs, trailblazing startup founders, and medical leaders shaping the future of longevity medicine, aesthetics, biohacking, and mental health. Follow Harris for her incisive perspective on the dynamic, rapidly evolving spaces of entrepreneurship, wellness, and lifestyle innovation.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6400d72ac69492121c55cb52/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=970\u0026cropY1=3\u0026cropY2=973","authorSlug":"meggenharris","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"Plastic is everywhere: in our buildings, clothing, food, cleaning and personal care products, and countless things we use every day. Scientists are now finding microscopic plastic particles throughout the human body, including the brain. What that means for our health is a much bigger question, and one researchers are only beginning to answer.","shortHeadline":"Microplastic Health Risks","topic":"Public Health","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/4251-6a790669008547865a71c6ab"},{"title":"How Azature Pogosian Bet On Anti-Scale Strategy And Won","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/stephanrabimov/2026/08/09/how-azature-pogosian-bet-on-anti-scale-strategy-and-won/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a79454ee35302ff50c30ffe/960x0.jpg","author":"Stephan Rabimov","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/2047632","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/stephanrabimov/","authorBio":"I’ve worked as a journalist, in digital and print publishing, public relations and marketing for over a decade. I have written and reported extensively about fashion and emerging markets, as well as contemporary culture, lifestyle, diplomacy, education, and the arts. I am a founder and editor-in-chief of DEPESHA magazine, and successfully launched Thom Browne, Rubin Singer, and Custo Barcelona brands in Russia, and Nicola Formichetti’s pop-up store in NYC. I have been interviewed and published across dozens of publications, including The New York Times Russia Beyond The Headlines Edition, Forbes International, The Huffington Post, Women's Wear Daily, L'Officiel, Vogue, Wall Street Journal, CNN, Yahoo Style, and Examiner. For Forbes.com I write about emerging fashion markets. Email me stephan.rabimov@gmail.com to be a part of my world.","authorAvatar":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/f336d56304b935f5ac961c9b52ccb88a?s=400\u0026d=mm\u0026r=g","authorSlug":"stephanrabimov","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"The global luxury jewelry market is rapidly expanding, projected to exceed $580 billion by decade's end, increasingly leveraging digital strategies and accessibility. Yet, Los Angeles jeweler Azature Pogosian, dubbed the \"Black Diamond King,\" built his brand on a contrasting philosophy. For nearly 25 years, he has prioritized deep relationships, bespoke craftsmanship, and organic word-of-mouth over mass reach and extensive ad spend. His accidental journey began with a $5,000 investment from his uncle, steering him from fashion into jewelry. Pogosian rose to prominence by transforming overlooked black diamonds into highly coveted pieces, notably gaining traction when Rihanna wore his designs. Pogosian defines true luxury by craftsmanship, the time invested, and the personal meaning of an \"eternal heirloom,\" not merely its cost. He consistently rejects mass distribution, asserting fine jewelry is too personal for off-the-shelf sales. This distinctive approach allows him to thrive amidst a diamond market increasingly shaped by lab-grown stones, emphasizing lasting value and legacy.","shortHeadline":"Entrepreneurial Success","topic":"Entrepreneurship","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/4398-6a793fb63358f564a50b8e23"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/FpeLxCUg-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"FpeLxCUg","audioDurationS":232,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-08-10T06:30:40-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-08-09-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-08-09","script":"Welcome to The Daily Brief, the essential news digest from the Forbes newsroom. It's Sunday, August 9. Today, we're exploring the stories shaping the global conversation—from the latest in Sports Policy and Geopolitics to a deep dive into Entertainment.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nWe begin with a look at, Ex-NBA players say they’re ‘declaring’ for WNBA as league considers transgender player policy. Mary Whitfill Roeloffs has been following this story and reports that\n\nFormer NBA players Royce White and Enes Kanter Freedom have provocatively declared their intent to enter the 2027 WNBA Draft, challenging the league's eligibility standards for transgender athletes. Kanter, who legally changed his name to include \"Freedom,\" stated he reviewed the WNBA's framework and believes he qualifies under \"self-identification and inclusion\" rules. White, who played briefly in the NBA, echoed this, telling Fox News he \"identifies as sometimes identifying as a woman\" for basketball purposes, claiming he'd be \"unstoppable.\" Both assert their actions are not to mock, but to push the WNBA to clarify its guidelines on who can play. This move intensifies a debate sparked by Indiana Fever star Sophie Cunningham, who opposed transgender women in the league, and further fueled by OutKick's interaction with French transgender player Julie Tétart. The WNBA's Collective Bargaining Agreement states \"Only players who are women are eligible,\" but lacks a definition for \"woman\" or a clause for eligibility based solely on self-identification. WNBA Commissioner Cathy Engelbert announced a task force to discuss the issue, emphasizing preserving game integrity. The WNBA Players Association, meanwhile, stressed diversity and inclusion, rejecting being used as \"political pawns\" and condemning hate against transgender people. Kanter summarized his position, stating, \"If simply declaring who you are is all that’s required, then I meet every single requirement necessary to compete in the WNBA.\"\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou can find the full analysis of that story at Forbes.com. Now, we're turning our attention to, Iran says U.S. must meet demands before strait of hormuz reopens, a development that could have a lasting impact on Geopolitics.\n\nIran has presented a comprehensive list of demands to the United States, declaring it will not reopen the vital Strait of Hormuz to marine traffic until these conditions are met. Mohammad Bagher Zolghadr, head of Iran’s Supreme National Security Council, outlined these requirements, which were published by Iranian media on Saturday. Key demands include the lifting of the U.S. naval blockade, an end to all sanctions, and the withdrawal of American troops from the region. Iran also insists the U.S. cease threatening the country, halt strikes in Iran, Lebanon, Palestine, Yemen, and Iraq permanently, withdraw all naval and air forces, pay war reparations, and release frozen Iranian assets. These stringent conditions significantly complicate ongoing talks between Iran and Oman, which were previously expected to lead to the waterway's reopening and a subsequent relaxation of global energy prices. Iran's foreign minister, Abbas Araghchi, confirmed that while an agreement with Oman was \"very close,\" the Strait's reopening remains contingent on the U.S. fulfilling these demands. However, American officials have consistently stated that sanctions relief is tied to a nuclear deal, and the release of frozen assets requires Iran to abandon its highly enriched uranium program. The Trump administration had not publicly commented on Iran's latest demands as of Saturday afternoon, with observers noting the U.S. is highly unlikely to agree to such extensive concessions.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nRounding out today's briefing, we look at, Where is macy gray now? the ‘I try’ singer is still full of surprises. This story is currently at the center of the conversation in Entertainment.\n\nMacy Gray, iconic for her 1999 hit \"I Try\" and distinctive raspy voice, recently delivered a confident and joyful performance at the Blue Note in Los Angeles. Her path to stardom was challenging; initially teased for her voice, she entered music accidentally after studying screenwriting. An early record deal with Atlantic failed, coinciding with her marriage's end, prompting her return to Ohio with her children, nearly abandoning music. However, executive Jeff Blue rediscovered her unreleased tracks, signing her under the alias \"Mushroom\" to secure a new deal with Epic Records in 1998. Her debut album, *On How Life Is*, eventually launched her to international fame with \"I Try.\" Now 58, Gray's voice has deepened, reflecting a seasoned artist who has shed her earlier shyness. Beyond music, she has acted in films like *Training Day*, covered Stevie Wonder's *Talking Book*, and appeared on *The Masked Singer*. Gray is scheduled to tour Australia, New Zealand, and Europe in 2026, with a new album, \"The Trouble With Truth,\" expected this fall, showcasing her enduring artistic vitality.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou've reached the end of this Daily Brief. Tune in again for tomorrow's updates. Thanks for joining us.","scriptTemplateId":0,"articles":[{"title":"Ex-NBA Players Say They’re ‘Declaring’ For WNBA As League Considers Transgender Player Policy","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/maryroeloffs/2026/08/08/ex-nba-players-say-theyre-declaring-for-wnba-as-league-considers-transgender-player-policy/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a7782e392552d54efa146e4/960x0.jpg?cropX1=423\u0026cropX2=2624\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=1238","author":"Mary Whitfill Roeloffs","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/7236790","authorUrl":"https://www.forbes.com/sites/maryroeloffs","authorBio":"Mary Roeloffs is a Forbes reporter who covers breaking news with a frequent focus on the entertainment industry, streaming, pop culture, politics, public health and climate change. She joined Forbes in 2023 and lives in Rochester, N.Y. She’s covered Hollywood’s most contentious lawsuits, allegations against Sean “Diddy” Combs, the Trump administration’s controversies, how AI is changing the job market and the 2026 hantavirus outbreak. Roeloffs was included on Editor \u0026 Publisher Magazine’s “25 Under 30” list in 2023 and worked covering local news in the greater Boston area from 2017 to 2023. She graduated with a double major in political science and journalism from Northeastern University. Follow Roeloffs for continued coverage of streaming wars, pop culture news and trending topics.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6487561380bb7d9077f4b945/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=400\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=400","authorSlug":"maryroeloffs","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Former NBA players Royce White and Enes Kanter Freedom have provocatively declared their intent to enter the 2027 WNBA Draft, challenging the league's eligibility standards for transgender athletes. Kanter, who legally changed his name 