eaming, pop culture, politics, public health and climate change. She joined Forbes in 2023 and lives in Rochester, N.Y. She’s covered Hollywood’s most contentious lawsuits, allegations against Sean “Diddy” Combs, the Trump administration’s controversies, how AI is changing the job market and the 2026 hantavirus outbreak. Roeloffs was included on Editor \u0026 Publisher Magazine’s “25 Under 30” list in 2023 and worked covering local news in the greater Boston area from 2017 to 2023. She graduated with a double major in political science and journalism from Northeastern University. Follow Roeloffs for continued coverage of streaming wars, pop culture news and trending topics.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6487561380bb7d9077f4b945/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=400\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=400","authorSlug":"maryroeloffs","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Iran has presented a comprehensive list of demands to the United States, declaring it will not reopen the vital Strait of Hormuz to marine traffic until these conditions are met. Mohammad Bagher Zolghadr, head of Iran’s Supreme National Security Council, outlined these requirements, which were published by Iranian media on Saturday. Key demands include the lifting of the U.S. naval blockade, an end to all sanctions, and the withdrawal of American troops from the region. Iran also insists the U.S. cease threatening the country, halt strikes in Iran, Lebanon, Palestine, Yemen, and Iraq permanently, withdraw all naval and air forces, pay war reparations, and release frozen Iranian assets. These stringent conditions significantly complicate ongoing talks between Iran and Oman, which were previously expected to lead to the waterway's reopening and a subsequent relaxation of global energy prices. Iran's foreign minister, Abbas Araghchi, confirmed that while an agreement with Oman was \"very close,\" the Strait's reopening remains contingent on the U.S. fulfilling these demands. However, American officials have consistently stated that sanctions relief is tied to a nuclear deal, and the release of frozen assets requires Iran to abandon its highly enriched uranium program. The Trump administration had not publicly commented on Iran's latest demands as of Saturday afternoon, with observers noting the U.S. is highly unlikely to agree to such extensive concessions.","shortHeadline":"Hormuz Strait Standoff","topic":"Geopolitics","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51951-6a77526ab5ddab35f0330d93"},{"title":"Where Is Macy Gray Now? The ‘I Try’ Singer Is Still Full Of Surprises","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/davidhochman/2026/08/08/where-is-macy-gray-now-the-i-try-singer-is-still-full-of-surprises/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a7751d1dcdff9c9dc44a4da/960x0.jpg","author":"David Hochman","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/548308","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/davidhochman/","authorBio":"David Hochman is an award-winning journalist providing a worldview from Los Angeles on luxury, travel, and entertainment. A Forbes writer (in print and online) since the early aughts, he explores epic experiences, unique destinations, luxury products, and profiles of passionate personalities in these fields--as in this Forbes print article on traveling the world by private jet. But concerts and culinary popups at iconic L.A. venues are also his jam. His bylines surface in The New York Times, GQ, Food + Wine, Town + Country and other venues. Hochman graduated from Columbia Journalism School with a Pulitzer traveling fellowship, and from Vassar College as an English major. Follow Hochman for continued coverage on what's delicious, refreshing, inspiring, indulgent and entertaining in California and way beyond.","authorAvatar":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/b2b9b8bc60dbb3da2e6e2db6e64bb5a9?s=400\u0026d=mm\u0026r=g","authorSlug":"davidhochman","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"Macy Gray, iconic for her 1999 hit \"I Try\" and distinctive raspy voice, recently delivered a confident and joyful performance at the Blue Note in Los Angeles. Her path to stardom was challenging; initially teased for her voice, she entered music accidentally after studying screenwriting. An early record deal with Atlantic failed, coinciding with her marriage's end, prompting her return to Ohio with her children, nearly abandoning music. However, executive Jeff Blue rediscovered her unreleased tracks, signing her under the alias \"Mushroom\" to secure a new deal with Epic Records in 1998. Her debut album, *On How Life Is*, eventually launched her to international fame with \"I Try.\" Now 58, Gray's voice has deepened, reflecting a seasoned artist who has shed her earlier shyness. Beyond music, she has acted in films like *Training Day*, covered Stevie Wonder's *Talking Book*, and appeared on *The Masked Singer*. Gray is scheduled to tour Australia, New Zealand, and Europe in 2026, with a new album, \"The Trouble With Truth,\" expected this fall, showcasing her enduring artistic vitality.","shortHeadline":"Macy Gray Update","topic":"Entertainment","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/2425-6a7744f6218830a7da9bee32"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/16Mv8kLS-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"16Mv8kLS","audioDurationS":326,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-08-09T08:30:52-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-08-08-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-08-08","script":"Welcome to The Daily Brief, a look at today's top reporting from Forbes. It's Saturday, August 8. On the agenda today: the latest developments in Politics, Politics, and Fashion.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nDiving into the Politics space, we start with, Max miller rally cancelled over ‘threats’ amid abuse allegations and calls for his resignation. According to reporting from Sara Dorn\n\nA local Ohio Republican group, the Strongsville GOP, cancelled a rally featuring Rep. Max Miller and Rep. Anna Paulina Luna, citing security concerns over \"the number and nature of the threats received.\" This cancellation follows a tumultuous week for Miller, who faces renewed allegations of abusing his ex-wife, Emily Miller, the daughter of Senator Bernie Moreno. Moreno has publicly called for Miller's resignation, labeling him a \"danger\" and claiming he needs \"serious psychological help.\" At least six Republican senators have joined Moreno in demanding Miller step down, prompting a House Ethics investigation. The controversy intensified after Miller posted a video denying the abuse claims, which he attributes to leverage in a custody dispute. Moreno also accused Miller of withholding his granddaughter's beloved blue bunny, an allegation Miller denies, though evidence suggests a history of this behavior. Despite the mounting pressure, Miller remains defiant, announcing plans to loan his re-election campaign $1 million. The rally, intended to support President Trump's voter ID law, was scheduled for the same day as Miller's deadline to resign from the ballot. While Trump has publicly defended Miller, he reportedly told him privately that the scandal could jeopardize his re-election chances.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nFor more context on that story, visit Forbes.com. Next, we shift our focus to, Blanche confirmation gets key vote for approval, a story that could have significant implications for Politics.\n\nSenator Lisa Murkowski, R-Alaska, announced she will vote against Todd Blanche’s confirmation as attorney general, making Senator Bill Cassidy, R-Louisiana, the deciding factor. Murkowski cited her inability to trust Blanche to \"check the worst impulses of this administration,\" pointing to Justice Department decisions she opposed, including the handling of Epstein files and immunity granted to Trump. With all Democrats and Senator Susan Collins, R-Maine, already opposing Blanche, and Senate Minority Leader Mitch McConnell, R-Kentucky, absent, Blanche can only afford to lose one more vote. Cassidy, who has become more critical of Trump since losing his primary, remains undecided, questioning if Blanche would serve independently or as Trump's personal attorney. The Senate Judiciary Committee advanced Blanche's nomination after he agreed to kill a controversial $1.8 billion anti-weaponization fund, though concerns remain it could be revived as Trump didn't sign the agreement. Collins also cited the IRS settlement and Blanche's opposition to restricting mail delivery of abortion pills for her opposition. The Senate's upcoming five-week recess could delay the confirmation, with McConnell's potential return from recovery possibly breaking a tie if Cassidy votes no. Blanche continues to serve as interim attorney general while his nomination is pending.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nOur final story today looks at, How john galliano’s 1994 ‘princess lucretia’ collection changed his fashion future forever. This remains a major point of conversation in the world of Fashion.\n\nJohn Galliano's Spring/Summer 1994 \"Princess Lucretia\" collection was a deeply personal and theatrical triumph, marking his comeback after significant financial hardship. Known for his narrative-driven haute couture, Galliano wove together diverse inspirations: a *Vanity Fair* article on the Romanovs, their royal DNA links, Tolstoy's *Anna Karenina*, and Madeleine Vionnet's bias cuts. The show itself was a spectacle, featuring supermodels like Kate Moss and Naomi Campbell who walked for free, responding to wolf howls in the soundtrack. Invitations were tea-stained scrolls, leading guests to a stark Louvre runway that starkly contrasted with the opulent, modified ball gowns, panniers, and micro kilts. This decadent, fairytale-inspired presentation, a testament to Galliano's \"enfant terrible\" brilliance, captivated fashion elites including Anna Wintour. Its success, earning him British Designer of the Year, directly propelled him to become the first British designer to lead Givenchy in 1995, solidifying his place in fashion history.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou're all caught up with The Daily Brief. Join us again tomorrow for your next news digest. Thanks for joining us.","scriptTemplateId":2,"articles":[{"title":"Max Miller Rally Cancelled Over ‘Threats’ Amid Abuse Allegations And Calls For His Resignation","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/08/07/max-miller-rally-cancelled-over-threats-amid-abuse-allegations-and-calls-for-his-resignation/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a7643036a7cac5185f362c5/960x0.jpg","author":"Sara Dorn","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/5010031","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn","authorBio":"Sara Dorn is a Forbes news reporter who covers politics, with a focus on elections and Capitol Hill. She joined Forbes in 2022 and is based in New York. She's covered the 2022 midterms, 2024 presidential campaign, the January 6 House committee investigation, former House Speaker Kevin McCarthy's historic ouster, the 2023 State of the Union Address, former President Donald Trump's federal election interference and classified documents cases and his Manhattan hush money case. Dorn graduated in 2012 from the University of Dayton with a degree in journalism. Prior to joining Forbes, she covered New York City and state politics for the New York Post and City and State magazine. Follow her for updates and analysis on the 2024 presidential race, key Senate and House races and developments in Congress and at the White House.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/63457b718c2404f82daa3149/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1284\u0026cropY1=403\u0026cropY2=1687","authorSlug":"saradorn","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"A local Ohio Republican group, the Strongsville GOP, cancelled a rally featuring Rep. Max Miller and Rep. Anna Paulina Luna, citing security concerns over \"the number and nature of the threats re