onstrating the enduring appeal of expertly balanced peat smoke, coastal influence, and long maturation. Kilchoman's Rockside 11 Years Old highlighted its unique farm-to-bottle style, while Lagavulin 16-Year-Old exemplified its rich peat smoke and maritime elegance. These award-winning whiskies underscore the individuality and world-class quality defining Scotland's western whisky regions. Glen Scotia's performance confirms its growing reputation for refined maritime malts, while Kilchoman and Lagavulin solidify Islay's status as the home of Scotland's most expressive peated whiskies, showcasing diverse and characterful styles.","shortHeadline":"Top Scotch Whiskies","topic":"Fine Spirits","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/5354-6a68dfb6bcd78378c6fb75a0"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/ZThe3snJ-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"ZThe3snJ","audioDurationS":272,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-08-06T06:30:37-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-08-05-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-08-05","script":"Welcome to The Daily Brief, a look at today's top reporting from Forbes. It's Wednesday, August 5. On the agenda today: the latest developments in Politics, Lobbying, and Astronomy.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nDiving into the Politics space, we start with, Bernie moreno accuses max miller of holding granddaughter’s bunny ‘hostage’. According to reporting from Sara Dorn\n\nSenator Bernie Moreno, R-Ohio, publicly lambasted Representative Max Miller, R-Ohio, on Tuesday, detailing a contentious custody exchange where Miller allegedly refused to return Moreno's granddaughter's beloved \"little blue bunny.\" Moreno described the incident as an act of \"total and complete depravity,\" stating Miller \"held it hostage for 48 hours,\" causing the two-year-old significant distress. The custody transfer, which Moreno handled at a suburban Cleveland police station due to fears about Miller's behavior, underscores the ongoing, heated dispute between Moreno's daughter and Miller. Moreno reiterated his call for Miller's resignation, asserting that Miller \"fails to meet that minimum basic standard\" for public office and that his daughter \"lives in constant fear of this man.\" This public escalation follows Miller's recent video statement denying abuse allegations from Moreno's daughter. Further developments Tuesday included Miller's attorney accepting responsibility for publishing an unclothed photo of their daughter in documents Miller released to support his innocence claims. Additionally, the House Ethics Committee announced an investigation into Miller, a probe he had requested himself to clear his name amidst the allegations.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nFor more context on that story, visit Forbes.com. Next, we shift our focus to, Sean duffy’s road trip sponsors spent $100 million lobbying congress last year, a story that could have significant implications for Lobbying.\n\nSean Duffy's \"The Great American Road Trip\" YouTube series, intended to inspire travel for America's 250th anniversary, remains unreleased despite a promoted June launch. Corporate sponsors, including Boeing and Toyota who each contributed $1 million, collectively spent an estimated $5 million on the project. These same companies, however, spent over $100 million lobbying Congress in 2025. The delay has drawn critics who highlight that many sponsors are regulated by Duffy's Department of Transportation, raising concerns about potential conflicts of interest and corporate influence.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nOur final story today looks at, How a village of 66 in spain is preparing for the eclipse — by closing its mountain. This remains a major point of conversation in the world of Astronomy.\n\nSpain is preparing for its first total solar eclipse since 1905 on August 12, with a tiny village at the center of a debate over sustainable tourism. Jabaloyas, home to just 66 residents, boasts El Javalón, a highly sought-after viewing location due to the eclipse's low position in the sky. However, its mayor has controversially decided to close vehicle access to the peak, prioritizing public safety and environmental protection over potential economic gain. The decision stems from fears that the village's limited infrastructure cannot handle thousands of visitors, posing risks like traffic gridlock preventing emergency services and increased wildfire danger in the UNESCO-recognized Valle del Cabriel Biosphere Reserve. The mayor, Óscar Castillo, emphasized that the mountain road was not built for such traffic, and a small municipality lacks resources for safe management. While acknowledging the desire for local businesses to benefit, Castillo stressed that safety is paramount and \"not up for sale.\" Visitors can still walk to El Javalón, and other viewing spots exist, promoting a more sustainable approach to tourism. This event highlights the challenge of balancing global astrotourism with the preservation of small, remote communities, especially with two more eclipses following in 2027 and 2028.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou're all caught up with The Daily Brief. Join us again tomorrow for your next news digest. Thanks for joining us.","scriptTemplateId":2,"articles":[{"title":"Bernie Moreno Accuses Max Miller Of Holding Granddaughter’s Bunny ‘Hostage’","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/08/04/bernie-moreno-accuses-max-miller-of-holding-granddaughters-bunny-hostage/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a722c5a7ca992c012db0c9d/960x0.jpg","author":"Sara Dorn","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/5010031","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn","authorBio":"Sara Dorn is a Forbes news reporter who covers politics, with a focus on elections and Capitol Hill. She joined Forbes in 2022 and is based in New York. She's covered the 2022 midterms, 2024 presidential campaign, the January 6 House committee investigation, former House