ral House contests. Its strategy involves mobilizing Republican voters less likely to participate in non-presidential election years and executing a \"historic 'get out the vote' operation.\" This spending comes as America PAC faces scrutiny over a $1 million payment to Wisconsin voters last year, tied to a petition. Musk, the largest political donor in 2024, had previously denounced the GOP and vowed to start his own party, but has since mended ties with Trump. Republicans currently risk losing their slim majorities in Congress, partly due to Trump's declining poll numbers and voter dissatisfaction, with Democrats leading by eight points on the generic congressional ballot. Interestingly, Musk recently expressed regret for getting \"too involved in politics\" and indicated he would reduce future political spending.","shortHeadline":"Musk Political Donations","topic":"Campaign Finance","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51554-6a6ba178b8bc52d6b673383a"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/6XFfi6p6-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"6XFfi6p6","audioDurationS":295,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-07-31T06:30:43-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-07-30-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-07-30","script":"You're listening to The Daily Brief, your essential news update from Forbes. It's Thursday, July 30. Today, we're highlighting the key stories covering Immigration, Astronomy, and a closer look at Luxury Travel.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nLeading off with, TSA is helping ICE ramp up arrests at airports. Suzanne Rowan Kelleher has been tracking this development and reports that\n\nThe Transportation Security Administration and Immigration and Customs Enforcement are collaborating to arrest foreign visitors at airports who have overstayed their U.S. visas, sparking concerns over data privacy. The New York Times revealed enforcement actions in at least 15 airports recently, targeting individuals including spouses of Americans. A May 2025 memo, obtained by American Oversight, details an agreement for ICE to share \"information with TSA to identify individuals of interest,\" with TSA reciprocating by sharing unspecified \"data elements.\" This initiative aligns with the Trump administration's intensified deportation agenda, aiming for thousands of daily arrests. Plain-clothes ICE agents are confronting travelers at check-in and arrival gates, sometimes filmed by fellow passengers. Immigration attorneys note that even those legally in the U.S. and in the asylum process have been detained, a development described as \"very, very new.\"\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nRead the full report on that story at Forbes.com. Moving now to, In photos: full ‘buck moon’ rises to set up a total solar eclipse, a story that's currently shaping the landscape in Astronomy.\n\nThe Buck Moon, the eighth full moon of 2026, illuminated midsummer skies on Wednesday, July 29, offering photographers a classic warm-weather spectacle. Best viewed at moonrise just after sunset, this full moon appeared low in the Northern Hemisphere sky and often looked orange due to atmospheric scattering, an effect enhanced by the moon illusion. Its traditional name comes from male deer regrowing antlers. This celestial event served as a prelude to a dramatic August, featuring a total solar eclipse on August 12. This eclipse will be visible from parts of Greenland, Iceland, and Spain, with a significant partial eclipse seen across Western Europe and North America, requiring special glasses. Just hours after the solar eclipse, the annual Perseid meteor shower will peak, potentially offering 50 shooting stars per hour. The month concludes with a deep partial lunar eclipse on August 27, where 96% of the Sturgeon Moon will enter Earth's shadow, creating a reddish hue visible across North America.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nTo finish today's update, we're turning to, The new mykonos resort with a water limo and a basketball court carved from rock. This headline is generating significant interest within Luxury Travel.\n\nFouquet’s Mykonos, the first Greek hotel from Barrière, opened in June on Paraga Beach, featuring 60 suites and three villas. It masterfully blends Mykonos' high-energy scene with private luxury. Its subtle French-meets-Mediterranean design features low-volume buildings that integrate into the rugged landscape with rough stone, sandy plaster, and warm wood. Accommodations range from rooms to expansive villas, many with private pools or sea access. Amenities include an NBA-standard blond-wood basketball court, a 2,260 sq ft gym, and the Rock Spa, offering Dr. Barbara Sturm and Germaine de Capuccini treatments. Dining features ROKA Mykonos for contemporary Japanese, Éole for Greek-Mediterranean, and Le Bar for signature cocktails. Owner Ioannis Papalekas's art collection, including Dalí's *Triumphant Elephant*, adorns the hotel. A unique 'water limo' service offers complimentary hourly transfers to hotspots like Scorpios and Nammos, providing a glamorous alternative to island traffic and private excursions.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nThat wraps up this edition of The Daily Brief. Be sure to check back tomorrow for our next update. Thanks for listening.","scriptTemplateId":1,"articles":[{"title":"TSA Is Helping ICE Ramp Up Arrests At Airports","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/suzannerowankelleher/2026/07/29/tsa-help-ice-arrests-airports/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a6a317a4b6bb0c1acfcaae9/960x0.jpg","author":"Suzanne Rowan Kelleher","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/3340630","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/suzannerowankelleher/","authorBio":"Suzanne Rowan Kelleher covers travel for Forbes. Follow her on Bluesky @srkelleher.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/601b7235b35a3ce5939c28fe/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1080\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=1080","authorSlug":"suzannerowankelleher","authorT